rity:nz.High,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-config:agent:choose-llm",title:{id:"6SM-EC",message:"Which LLM should I pick for my agent?"},userMessage:{id:"a37c0c",message:"Help me choose the best LLM for my agent based on its complexity, latency needs and cost. Walk me through the tradeoffs and recommend a primary plus cascade backups."},contextInjection:"The user wants guidance choosing the best LLM. Walk them through the model tiers: 1) For simple single-node agents on telephony: gpt-4o-mini (~736ms, ~$0.002/min) or gpt-4.1-mini (~834ms) — fast enough, capable enough for basic tool calling, 2) For complex multi-node agents: gemini-2.5-flash (~1.05s, ~$0.002/min — best cost/quality balance), gpt-5.2 (~843ms), or gpt-4o (~743ms) — reliable edge transitions and tool execution, 3) For complex reasoning/judgment: claude-sonnet-4.6 (~937ms) or gpt-5.2, 4) Avoid nano/lite models (qwen3-30b-a3b, gpt-4.1-nano, gpt-5-nano) as primary models — they struggle with multi-step reasoning and complex tool schemas; use them only as cascade backups, 5) Set up a cascade with 3s timeout for telephony (5-8s for web) with cross-provider diversity (e.g., primary Gemini → backup OpenAI → backup Anthropic), 6) Test tool-calling reliability with their specific tool schemas before committing.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-config:agent:optimize-latency",title:{id:"wDfJb2",message:"Why is my agent slow?"},userMessage:{id:"U3H4-C",message:"Help me reduce my agent's response latency. Look at the TTS model, LLM choice, cascade timeout, prompt length and workflow shape, and recommend the highest-impact changes."},contextInjection:"The user wants to reduce their agent's response latency. Guide them through these steps in order of impact: 1) Switch TTS to eleven_flash_v2 for English telephony (lowest latency) or eleven_flash_v2_5 for multilingual — avoid V3 on telephony unless they need precise number pronunciation, 2) Choose a faster LLM: gemini-2.5-flash (~1.05s) or gpt-4o-mini (~736ms) instead of heavier models, 3) Set cascade_timeout_seconds to 3 for telephony so fallback kicks in fast, 4) Shorten the system prompt — long prompts increase first-token latency; move detailed instructions to node-level additional_prompt where they only load when needed, 5) If using workflows: minimize node count — each transition causes a full LLM brain teardown and rebuild (dead air); merge sequential phases into single nodes with internal phase labels, 6) For tool-heavy agents: reduce tool call chains and ensure tool response_timeout_secs is reasonable (default 20s), 7) Enable speculative turn to start generating before the user finishes speaking.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["get_workflow","update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-config:agent:multilingual",title:{id:"fxwcNU",message:"How do I make my agent multilingual?"},userMessage:{id:"Dqy-bt",message:"Help me configure my agent to support multiple languages, including TTS model choice, language settings, prompt language and voice selection."},contextInjection:"The user wants to configure their agent for multiple languages. Walk them through: 1) TTS model selection: eleven_flash_v2 is English-only — they must use eleven_flash_v2_5 (32 languages) or eleven_v3_conversational for multilingual, 2) Set conversation_config.agent.language to match the expected caller language, 3) For the system prompt: write instructions in English even for non-English agents — the LLM follows English instructions and responds in the configured language; only write prompts in the target language if instruction-following degrades, 4) Select appropriate voices for each language from the voice library, 5) For RAG/knowledge base: use multilingual_e5_large_instruct embedding model instead of the default e5_mistral_7b_instruct for non-English content, 6) Test with native speakers — digit pronunciation, date formats, and cultural conventions vary by language.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-config:agent:configure-v3-voice",title:{id:"ZKfpTv",message:"How do I set up the V3 conversational voice?"},userMessage:{id:"8N81j7",message:"Walk me through configuring the eleven_v3_conversational TTS model, including expressive mode, audio tags, anti-markup rules and known issues to watch for."},contextInjection:"The user wants to set up or troubleshoot the V3 conversational voice model (eleven_v3_conversational). Walk them through: 1) Select eleven_v3_conversational as the TTS model — it supports ~70 languages with more natural, expressive speech than V2 models, but is slightly slower, 2) Enable expressive mode in TTS settings — this adds a system prompt telling the LLM it can use audio tags like [happy], [sad], [whisper], [dramatic], 3) Add audio tags to the first_message (e.g., '[friendly] Welcome to...') — this signals to the LLM that tags are acceptable, 4) Configure suggested_audio_tags (up to 20) to guide which emotions the agent can express, and explain to the LLM when to use each tag in the system prompt, 5) CRITICAL: V3 reads EVERYTHING aloud — never output HTML tags, SSML, markdown, or any markup. Add anti-markup instructions to the prompt, 6) Common issues: audio tags being read aloud instead of interpreted (try removing tags and expressive mode as a workaround — V3 still sounds better than V2 without tags), volume dropping over time (caused by repeated emotion tags like [calm] stacking), garbled audio loops (known V3 issue — restart the conversation or switch to a V2 model as a workaround), accent switching mid-conversation (usually caused by a voice trained before V3 was released — retrain the voice), 7) V3 does NOT support speed/stability sliders — use prompt instructions like 'speak slowly' or 'speak quickly' instead, 8) For telephony: eleven_flash_v2 is still lower latency for English-only — only use V3 if you need precise number pronunciation or expressiveness.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-config:agent:setup-env-vars",title:{id:"n39Yrb",message:"How do I use environment variables across staging and production?"},userMessage:{id:"GepL0q",message:"Help me set up environment variables so I can use one agent for both staging and production, including how to reference them in webhook URLs, MCP servers, headers and tool params."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user wants to configure environment variables for their agent. Walk them through: 1) Environment variables let you use different values for staging vs production — webhook URLs, MCP server URLs, API keys, and auth headers can all reference environment variables, 2) Three types: StringEnvironmentVariable (plain key-value), SecretEnvironmentVariable (sensitive values stored via secret_id), and AuthConnectionEnvironmentVariable (OAuth connections like Google or Slack), 3) Reference variables in tool configs using {{env__VARIABLE_NAME}} template syntax — this works in webhook URLs, MCP server URLs, request headers, and tool parameters, 4) Create environment variables via the Developers tab or API (PATCH/POST /agents/environment-variables), 5) At runtime, the platform resolves the variable to the value matching the current environment (staging or production), 6) Use this to maintain a SINGLE agent definition for both staging and production — no need to duplicate agents. Set the environment parameter when starting a conversation to control which values resolve, 7) For secrets: use SecretEnvironmentVariable with a secret_id reference — this keeps API keys out of the agent JSON config.",priority:nz.Medium},{id:"agent-config:agent:setup-transfers",title:{id:"8zeqOX",message:"How do I transfer calls to a human?"},userMessage:{id:"_qGW_x",message:"Help me set up phone transfers. Compare the transfer_to_number system tool vs the Phone Transfer node, and recommend which to use for my agent."},contextInjection:"The user wants their agent to transfer calls to humans. Walk them through the two approaches and help them choose: 1) transfer_to_number system tool (RECOMMENDED): add it to the agent's tools with transfer destinations, conditions, and transfer_type ('blind' for cold transfer, 'conference' for warm — never 'warm' or 'cold', those are invalid). If the transfer fails, control returns to the agent so it can retry or take a message. Reference it in the prompt: 'If caller asks for a human, use transfer_to_number', 2) Phone Transfer node (simple but no fallback): add a phone_number node to the workflow — this is TERMINAL, no outgoing edges allowed, and if the transfer fails the call just ends, 3) For transfers from anywhere in the conversation: put the transfer_to_number tool on the base agent and add instructions in the root prompt. For transfers only from specific phases: scope the tool to the relevant node via additional_tool_ids, 4) For multiple departments: add multiple transfer entries with different conditions and phone numbers, 5) For dynamic routing: use phone_dynamic_variable type with {{department_phone}} populated by a prior tool call.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config","create_tool"]},{id:"agent-config:agent:custom-llm",title:{id:"jENyPY",message:"How do I connect a custom LLM endpoint?"},userMessage:{id:"-E27c_",message:"Walk me through configuring a custom OpenAI-compatible LLM endpoint, including authentication, tool calling support and the limitations to watch for."},contextInjection:"The user wants to configure a custom LLM endpoint. Walk them through: 1) Prepare an OpenAI Chat Completions-compatible API endpoint URL, 2) Configure authentication (API key or Bearer token) in the agent settings, 3) Set the model identifier and parameters (temperature, max tokens), 4) Important limitation: custom LLMs cannot be used as cascade backups — set up ElevenLabs-hosted models as fallbacks, 5) Test tool calling: custom endpoints must support the OpenAI function-calling format for tools to work, 6) Test with a sample conversation before going live, 7) Monitor for latency — self-hosted endpoints may have variable response times that affect telephony quality.",priority:nz.Low,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config"]}],nq=[{id:"agent-config:kb:improve-rag",title:{id:"PiGkuB",message:"How do I improve this agent's retrieval accuracy?"},userMessage:{id:"JcsF_7",message:"Help me improve retrieval for this agent's knowledge base. Walk me through how RAG works here and the changes I should make to headings, chunk size, duplicates and retrieval thresholds."},contextInjection:"This agent's knowledge base isn't returning accurate results. Walk them through the RAG pipeline internals and optimization: 1) Use question-style headings in documents (e.g., '## What medications are available?') — the retrieval query is a rewritten question, so Q-style headings dramatically increase embedding similarity, 2) Keep articles to 1.5-3 KB — this maps to 1-2 chunks with the default e5_mistral_7b_instruct model (1024 tokens ≈ 4KB), making each chunk more likely to contain a complete answer, 3) Minimize duplicate content across articles — duplicates waste the 20-chunk retrieval slots with redundant results, 4) Remove YAML front matter — RAG retrieval is pure embedding similarity, metadata becomes noise in the first chunk, 5) Adjust max_vector_distance (default 0.6) — lower values are stricter, higher values retrieve more but less relevant chunks, 6) Cross-references between articles don't work — the LLM only sees retrieved chunks, not referenced articles; if context from another article is needed, that content must appear in a chunk that gets retrieved on its own merit, 7) If this agent uses per-node KB scoping via additional_knowledge_base, check the right documents are assigned to the right nodes.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["list_knowledge_base","update_document","update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-config:kb:cant-find-answers",title:{id:"bnC1vw",message:"Why can't this agent find the right answers?"},userMessage:{id:"w7vkAd",message:"This agent isn't finding the right answers in its knowledge base. Help me debug the RAG pipeline step by step and pinpoint what to fix."},contextInjection:"This agent is failing to find correct answers from its knowledge base. Debug the RAG pipeline step by step: 1) Verify documents are uploaded and processed — check the knowledge base tab for processing status, 2) Understand how retrieval works: the user's message is rewritten into a self-contained question by an LLM, then matched against chunk embeddings via vector similarity — document names, folder paths, and metadata are NOT used in search, 3) Check if the answer exists within a single chunk — the chunker splits on paragraph breaks and sentence endings, not on logical sections, so answers that span multiple paragraphs may be split across chunks, 4) Structure documents so answers are self-contained within ~1.5-3 KB sections, 5) Test the exact user phrasing against the knowledge base to see what chunks are returned, 6) If using per-node KB scoping via additional_knowledge_base: verify the right documents are assigned to the right nodes for this agent.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["list_knowledge_base","list_conversations","get_conversation_detail"]}],nV=[{id:"kb-page:improve-rag",title:{id:"kFB9QY",message:"How do I improve knowledge base retrieval?"},userMessage:{id:"fR3YJ5",message:"Help me improve retrieval quality across my workspace knowledge base. Walk me through how RAG works here and the changes to make to headings, chunk size, duplicates and retrieval thresholds."},contextInjection:"The user is on the workspace knowledge base page (documents shared across many agents) and wants better retrieval quality. Walk them through the RAG pipeline internals and optimization: 1) Use question-style headings in documents (e.g., '## What medications are available?') — the retrieval query is a rewritten question, so Q-style headings dramatically increase embedding similarity, 2) Keep articles to 1.5-3 KB — this maps to 1-2 chunks with the default e5_mistral_7b_instruct model (1024 tokens ≈ 4KB), making each chunk more likely to contain a complete answer, 3) Minimize duplicate content across articles — duplicates waste the 20-chunk retrieval slots with redundant results, 4) Remove YAML front matter — RAG retrieval is pure embedding similarity, metadata becomes noise in the first chunk, 5) Adjust max_vector_distance (default 0.6) — lower values are stricter, higher values retrieve more but less relevant chunks, 6) Cross-references between articles don't work — the LLM only sees retrieved chunks, not referenced articles, 7) Since a document here can be attached to many agents, structure it to stand alone rather than assuming one agent's context.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["list_knowledge_base","update_document"]},{id:"kb-page:organize-documents",title:{id:"5xdqMj",message:"How should I organize my knowledge base?"},userMessage:{id:"uve2JX",message:"Help me organize my workspace knowledge base: which documents to share across agents, when to split content, deduping overlapping articles and naming for findability."},contextInjection:"The user is on the workspace knowledge base page and wants to organize documents that may be shared across multiple agents. Walk them through: 1) Decide what belongs in shared workspace documents (company-wide policies, product facts) vs per-agent documents — shared content should be agent-agnostic so it reads correctly no matter which agent retrieves it, 2) Split large documents so each answer is self-contained within ~1.5-3 KB, 3) Dedupe overlapping articles — duplicates across the workspace waste the 20-chunk retrieval slots and produce redundant results, 4) Remember retrieval is pure embedding similarity: document names and folder structure are NOT used in search, so put the searchable content (question-style headings) inside the document body, 5) For an agent that needs only a subset, attach specific documents to that agent (or per-node via additional_knowledge_base) rather than relying on naming.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["list_knowledge_base","update_document"]}],nG=[{id:"agent-config:tools:create-webhook",title:{id:"qJg49j",message:"How do I build a custom webhook tool?"},userMessage:{id:"rLSLvW",message:"Walk me through creating a webhook tool, including the schema rules for URLs, request body, headers, response assignments and error handling."},contextInjection:"The user wants to create a webhook tool. Walk them through the schema rules: 1) Define a clear tool name and description — the LLM uses the description to decide when to call it, so make it specific ('Look up a customer by their phone number' not 'Customer lookup'), 2) Set the webhook URL — URLs must be static, no {{variables}} in paths; move dynamic values to request_body_schema, 3) Define request_body_schema as a JSON Schema object with descriptive property names — each property needs exactly one of: description, constant_value, dynamic_variable, or is_system_provided (they're mutually exclusive), 4) For array properties: items schema is required and constant_value is not allowed on arrays, 5) Configure request_headers as an object (not array) — for secret API keys use {\"secret_id\": \"...\"} via GET /v1/convai/secrets, 6) Set response_timeout_secs (default 20, max 120), 7) To extract values from responses: add assignments with dot-notation value_path (e.g., 'data.items.0.id' not 'data.items[0].id'), 8) Add error handling in the node prompt: 'If tool_name returns an error, [fallback behavior]'.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["create_tool","update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-config:tools:debug",title:{id:"QzFfHs",message:"Why is my tool failing?"},userMessage:{id:"th2g8f",message:"Help me debug a failing tool. Walk me through checking the transcript, webhook URL, auth, request body, timeouts, response paths and tool scoping."},contextInjection:"The user has a tool that's failing. Debug step by step: 1) Check the conversation transcript for the exact error — look at the tool call and response in the call logs, 2) Verify the webhook URL is accessible and returns 200 — test it directly with the same parameters, 3) Check authentication: are credentials correct and not expired? For secret headers, verify the secret_id is still valid via GET /v1/convai/secrets, 4) Validate the request body format — common issues: missing required fields, wrong types, {{dynamic_variable}} not populated (variables only work with data from pre-call webhooks, SDK, or other tool responses — NOT from conversation content), 5) Check response_timeout_secs — if the API is slow, increase it (max 120s), 6) If using response assignments: verify the value_path uses dot notation (data.items.0.id) not bracket notation, 7) Check if tool scoping is correct — is the tool assigned to this node via additional_tool_ids? If tool_ids is set at the base agent level, verify the tool is included.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["list_tools","get_conversation_detail","list_conversations","update_tool"]},{id:"agent-config:tools:setup-transfers",title:{id:"8zeqOX",message:"How do I transfer calls to a human?"},userMessage:{id:"_qGW_x",message:"Help me set up phone transfers. Compare the transfer_to_number system tool vs the Phone Transfer node, and recommend which to use for my agent."},contextInjection:"The user wants their agent to transfer calls to humans. Walk them through the two approaches and help them choose: 1) transfer_to_number system tool (RECOMMENDED): add it to the agent's tools with transfer destinations, conditions, and transfer_type ('blind' for cold transfer, 'conference' for warm — never 'warm' or 'cold', those are invalid). If the transfer fails, control returns to the agent so it can retry or take a message. Reference it in the prompt: 'If caller asks for a human, use transfer_to_number', 2) Phone Transfer node (simple but no fallback): add a phone_number node to the workflow — this is TERMINAL, no outgoing edges allowed, and if the transfer fails the call just ends, 3) For transfers from anywhere in the conversation: put the transfer_to_number tool on the base agent and add instructions in the root prompt. For transfers only from specific phases: scope the tool to the relevant node via additional_tool_ids, 4) For multiple departments: add multiple transfer entries with different conditions and phone numbers, 5) For dynamic routing: use phone_dynamic_variable type with {{department_phone}} populated by a prior tool call.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["create_tool","update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-config:tools:setup-mcp",title:{id:"8lVkJr",message:"How do I connect an MCP server?"},userMessage:{id:"vl_z0r",message:"Walk me through connecting an MCP server to my agent, including transport type, authentication, tool naming, schema limitations and approval policies."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user wants to connect an MCP (Model Context Protocol) server to their agent. Walk them through: 1) MCP servers provide standardized tool discovery — the platform automatically fetches available tools from the server and exposes them to the agent, 2) Configure the server URL (HTTPS required except localhost), transport type (SSE or STREAMABLE_HTTP), and authentication (secret_token for Bearer auth OR auth_connection for OAuth — mutually exclusive), 3) Tool naming: MCP tools are flattened into the agent's tool list with a prefix format of '{server_id}:{tool_name}' — this can confuse the LLM. Add a prompt instruction telling the agent the exact tool names it should use, 4) Schema limitations: the platform strips most JSON Schema keywords (pattern, minimum, maximum, enum, default, examples) — only type, properties, required, description, and items are kept. If your tools use discriminated unions (oneOf), they may silently disappear. Move constraints into the tool description instead, 5) Tool overrides: use tool_config_overrides to set per-tool execution_mode, disable_interruptions, or force_pre_tool_speech, 6) Approval policies: AUTO_APPROVE_ALL (default), REQUIRE_APPROVAL_ALL, or REQUIRE_APPROVAL_PER_TOOL — for sensitive tools, require approval, 7) LATENCY WARNING: MCP servers add extra latency vs webhook tools due to the protocol overhead. For voice agents where latency matters, prefer webhook tools for simple API calls. Use MCP when you need the standardized tool discovery or complex tool ecosystems, 8) Use environment variables ({{env__MCP_URL}}) in the server URL to switch between staging and production MCP servers without duplicating agents.",priority:nz.High},{id:"agent-config:tools:setup-client-tools",title:{id:"dfQrzC",message:"How do I add client-side tools?"},userMessage:{id:"JcsRpi",message:"Help me set up client-side tools that run in the browser via the widget or SDK. Cover parameters, expects_response, execution modes and the event integration pattern."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user wants to create client-side tools that execute in the browser/app via the widget or SDK. Walk them through: 1) Client tools let the agent trigger actions in the user's browser — UI navigation, opening dialogs, reading on-screen context, cache invalidation, or any arbitrary JavaScript, 2) Create a client tool with a clear name and description. Define parameters as JSON Schema properties — these are passed to the widget when the tool is called, 3) Key setting: expects_response — if true, the agent waits for the client to return a result before continuing (up to response_timeout_secs, max 120s). If false, it's fire-and-forget, 4) Execution modes: 'immediate' (tool runs right away), 'post_tool_speech' (agent speaks first, then tool runs), 'async' (fire-and-forget, no waiting), 5) Widget integration: listen for ClientToolCall events on the websocket/event stream. Each event includes tool_call_id, tool_name, and parameters. Execute your logic, then send back a ClientToolResult with the same tool_call_id and the result string, 6) Use cases from office hours: navigate users to specific app pages, open/close dialogs, read current page context to provide relevant help, invalidate client-side caches when server data changes, access error stack traces for troubleshooting, 7) For cache invalidation: monitor the event stream for server-side MCP tool calls — when data is updated, invalidate your client query cache, 8) Error handling: if the client doesn't respond within response_timeout_secs, the agent receives a CLIENT_TIMEOUT error and can decide to retry or fall back. Duplicate responses for the same tool_call_id are ignored.",priority:nz.Medium},{id:"agent-config:tools:voicemail-detection",title:{id:"DIE-pG",message:"How do I handle voicemail on outbound calls?"},userMessage:{id:"x4dPxc",message:"Help me set up voicemail handling for my agent, including the voicemail_detection system tool for outbound calls and what happens during transfer-to-human voicemail."},contextInjection:"The user wants to handle voicemail scenarios. Walk them through the two distinct voicemail situations: 1) OUTBOUND VOICEMAIL DETECTION: Add the voicemail_detection system tool to the agent. When making outbound calls, this tool detects if a voicemail system answers instead of a human. The agent can then hang up (to avoid talking to a voicemail) or leave a message with dynamic variable interpolation (e.g., 'Hi {{customer_name}}, this is...'), 2) TRANSFER-TO-HUMAN VOICEMAIL: When using transfer_to_number to transfer a call to a human agent, if the human doesn't answer and it goes to voicemail, the current behavior depends on transfer type. With 'conference' transfer: the platform currently loses control of the call once transferred — it cannot pull the caller back. With 'blind' transfer: the call is fully handed off. The team is working on solutions to detect voicemail during transfers and return control to the agent, 3) For outbound IVR navigation: combine voicemail_detection with the play_keypad_touch_tone system tool for DTMF navigation. Set suppress_turn_after_dtmf: true and disable_interruptions: true, 4) Current limitation: voicemail detection logic is relatively simple — it works for standard voicemail greetings but may not catch all edge cases like iPhone call screening, 5) Add skip_turn tool alongside voicemail detection — if the agent encounters hold music or automated prompts while waiting, skip_turn prevents the agent from talking over them or disconnecting.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["create_tool","update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-config:tools:connect-api",title:{id:"-6eOW2",message:"How do I connect my agent to an external API?"},userMessage:{id:"ADxC77",message:"Help me connect my agent to an external API. Walk me through building a webhook tool, mapping params to the request body, configuring auth and using response data in the conversation."},contextInjection:"The user wants to connect their agent to an external API. Walk them through: 1) Get the API documentation — identify the endpoint URL, HTTP method, auth method, and request/response format, 2) Create a webhook tool: set the static base URL (no {{variables}} in the path), 3) Map API parameters to request_body_schema — use JSON Schema with descriptive property fields, 4) Configure auth headers: for API keys use request_headers with either plain values or secret_id references for workspace secrets, 5) To use response data in conversation: add assignments that map response fields to dynamic variables (e.g., source: 'response', value_path: 'data.customer.name', dynamic_variable: 'customer_name'), 6) Add usage instructions in the node prompt — tell the agent exactly when to call the tool and how to speak the results naturally, 7) Handle errors in the prompt: 'If [tool_name] returns an error or no results, apologize and offer alternatives', 8) Test with sample data before going live.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["create_tool","update_agent_config"]}],nK=[{id:"tools-page:create-webhook",title:{id:"qJg49j",message:"How do I build a custom webhook tool?"},userMessage:{id:"rLSLvW",message:"Walk me through creating a webhook tool, including the schema rules for URLs, request body, headers, response assignments and error handling."},contextInjection:"The user wants to create a webhook tool. Walk them through the schema rules: 1) Define a clear tool name and description — the LLM uses the description to decide when to call it, so make it specific ('Look up a customer by their phone number' not 'Customer lookup'), 2) Set the webhook URL — URLs must be static, no {{variables}} in paths; move dynamic values to request_body_schema, 3) Define request_body_schema as a JSON Schema object with descriptive property names — each property needs exactly one of: description, constant_value, dynamic_variable, or is_system_provided (they're mutually exclusive), 4) For array properties: items schema is required and constant_value is not allowed on arrays, 5) Configure request_headers as an object (not array) — for secret API keys use {\"secret_id\": \"...\"} via GET /v1/convai/secrets, 6) Set response_timeout_secs (default 20, max 120), 7) To extract values from responses: add assignments with dot-notation value_path (e.g., 'data.items.0.id' not 'data.items[0].id'), 8) Add error handling in the node prompt: 'If tool_name returns an error, [fallback behavior]'.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["create_tool"]},{id:"tools-page:debug",title:{id:"QzFfHs",message:"Why is my tool failing?"},userMessage:{id:"th2g8f",message:"Help me debug a failing tool. Walk me through checking the transcript, webhook URL, auth, request body, timeouts, response paths and tool scoping."},contextInjection:"The user has a tool that's failing. Debug step by step: 1) Check the conversation transcript for the exact error — look at the tool call and response in the call logs, 2) Verify the webhook URL is accessible and returns 200 — test it directly with the same parameters, 3) Check authentication: are credentials correct and not expired? For secret headers, verify the secret_id is still valid via GET /v1/convai/secrets, 4) Validate the request body format — common issues: missing required fields, wrong types, {{dynamic_variable}} not populated (variables only work with data from pre-call webhooks, SDK, or other tool responses — NOT from conversation content), 5) Check response_timeout_secs — if the API is slow, increase it (max 120s), 6) If using response assignments: verify the value_path uses dot notation (data.items.0.id) not bracket notation, 7) Check if tool scoping is correct — is the tool assigned to this node via additional_tool_ids? If tool_ids is set at the base agent level, verify the tool is included.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["list_tools","update_tool"]},{id:"tools-page:setup-client-tools",title:{id:"dfQrzC",message:"How do I add client-side tools?"},userMessage:{id:"JcsRpi",message:"Help me set up client-side tools that run in the browser via the widget or SDK. Cover parameters, expects_response, execution modes and the event integration pattern."},contextInjection:"The user wants to create client-side tools that execute in the browser/app via the widget or SDK. Walk them through: 1) Client tools let the agent trigger actions in the user's browser — UI navigation, opening dialogs, reading on-screen context, cache invalidation, or any arbitrary JavaScript, 2) Create a client tool with a clear name and description. Define parameters as JSON Schema properties — these are passed to the widget when the tool is called, 3) Key setting: expects_response — if true, the agent waits for the client to return a result before continuing (up to response_timeout_secs, max 120s). If false, it's fire-and-forget, 4) Execution modes: 'immediate' (tool runs right away), 'post_tool_speech' (agent speaks first, then tool runs), 'async' (fire-and-forget, no waiting), 5) Widget integration: listen for ClientToolCall events on the websocket/event stream. Each event includes tool_call_id, tool_name, and parameters. Execute your logic, then send back a ClientToolResult with the same tool_call_id and the result string, 6) Use cases from office hours: navigate users to specific app pages, open/close dialogs, read current page context to provide relevant help, invalidate client-side caches when server data changes, access error stack traces for troubleshooting, 7) For cache invalidation: monitor the event stream for server-side MCP tool calls — when data is updated, invalidate your client query cache, 8) Error handling: if the client doesn't respond within response_timeout_secs, the agent receives a CLIENT_TIMEOUT error and can decide to retry or fall back. Duplicate responses for the same tool_call_id are ignored.",priority:nz.Medium},{id:"tools-page:connect-api",title:{id:"-6eOW2",message:"How do I connect my agent to an external API?"},userMessage:{id:"ADxC77",message:"Help me connect my agent to an external API. Walk me through building a webhook tool, mapping params to the request body, configuring auth and using response data in the conversation."},contextInjection:"The user wants to connect their agent to an external API. Walk them through: 1) Get the API documentation — identify the endpoint URL, HTTP method, auth method, and request/response format, 2) Create a webhook tool: set the static base URL (no {{variables}} in the path), 3) Map API parameters to request_body_schema — use JSON Schema with descriptive property fields, 4) Configure auth headers: for API keys use request_headers with either plain values or secret_id references for workspace secrets, 5) To use response data in conversation: add assignments that map response fields to dynamic variables (e.g., source: 'response', value_path: 'data.customer.name', dynamic_variable: 'customer_name'), 6) Add usage instructions in the node prompt — tell the agent exactly when to call the tool and how to speak the results naturally, 7) Handle errors in the prompt: 'If [tool_name] returns an error or no results, apologize and offer alternatives', 8) Test with sample data before going live.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["create_tool"]}];nz.High,nz.Medium;let n$=[{id:"agent-config:security:auth",title:{id:"qi-NJS",message:"How do I secure access to my agent?"},userMessage:{id:"6j47uk",message:"Help me set up authentication for my agent. Cover the right method for web widgets, API access and telephony, plus key rotation and CORS."},contextInjection:"The user wants to secure their agent. Walk them through the available methods: 1) For web widgets: signed URLs (recommended) — generate a signing key, create tokens server-side with user identity and expiration, pass to widget. Domain whitelisting as a simpler alternative, 2) For API access: API key authentication — create keys with appropriate scopes, store securely (env vars or secrets manager), 3) For telephony: configure allowed caller IDs or use pre-call webhooks to validate callers, 4) Set up key rotation: create the new key first, update all integrations, then revoke the old key — never revoke before updating, 5) Configure CORS and allowed origins, 6) Test that unauthenticated and unauthorized requests are properly rejected.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-config:security:call-limits",title:{id:"Q9vKhS",message:"How do I cap usage and prevent abuse?"},userMessage:{id:"vs-mmv",message:"Help me configure call limits to prevent abuse and control costs. Cover per-caller rate limits, daily limits, concurrent call limits and outbound limits."},contextInjection:"The user wants to prevent abuse and control costs with call limits. Walk them through: 1) Set per-caller rate limits to prevent individual abuse — this limits how many calls a single phone number or user can make in a time window, 2) Configure daily call limits as a cost ceiling — calculate based on their plan's included minutes and budget, 3) Set concurrent call limits based on their plan capacity — this prevents overload during traffic spikes, 4) For outbound/batch calling: set separate limits to prevent accidentally burning through minutes, 5) Set up post-call webhooks to monitor usage patterns and detect anomalies, 6) Consider setting silence_end_call_timeout (default disabled) to auto-disconnect silent calls that waste minutes — but be careful: agents on hold need skip_turn to prevent premature disconnection.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config"]}],nQ=[{id:"agent-config:guardrails:setup",title:{id:"2xDWuW",message:"How do I add safety guardrails?"},userMessage:{id:"89RGsK",message:"Help me configure guardrails for my agent on the Guardrails tab. Cover built-in vs custom guardrails, execution modes, exit strategies, and anything I should also set up in the system prompt."},contextInjection:"The user wants help configuring Guardrails 2.0 on the Guardrails tab. Focus the walkthrough on guardrail setup there, but briefly note that guardrails work best alongside a clear system prompt in the Agent tab — if goals, tone, or boundaries are missing from the prompt, fix that too; guardrails enforce policy, they do not replace prompt design. Walk them through: 1) In the Guardrails tab, enable the pre-built guardrails that fit their use case — Focus reinforces the system prompt and keeps responses on-task (especially in long conversations), Manipulation detects prompt-injection / instruction-override attempts, Content screens agent responses across sensitive categories with tunable sensitivity thresholds, 2) Add Custom guardrails for domain-specific policies in natural language (e.g. 'Never provide medical diagnoses', 'Do not discuss competitor products', 'Always recommend consulting a professional for legal questions') — a lightweight model evaluates each agent response independently and in parallel with generation, 3) Execution modes: run guardrails alongside the response for near-zero latency (a fraction of a second of audio may play before interception) or hold responses until fully cleared (slightly slower, nothing reaches the user unchecked), 4) Exit strategies when a guardrail triggers: end the conversation, transfer to another agent, escalate to a human, or retry with corrective instructions — help them pick the right option for their risk tolerance and UX, 5) Guardrails constrain what the agent says and does; handling abusive callers or off-topic users may still belong in the system prompt (e.g. politely end the call) — mention this only if relevant, 6) Use conversation analytics to see which guardrails fired and tune configuration over time, 7) For per-user dynamic boundaries (e.g. different rules by subscription tier): pass guardrail configuration via dynamic variables at conversation start through a secure server-to-server flow (signed session token or pre-call webhook).",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config"]}],nY=[{id:"agent-config:tests:write-tests",title:{id:"ikD0vw",message:"Can you draft test cases for my agent?"},userMessage:{id:"r9BnI3",message:"Generate a starter set of test cases for my agent covering the happy path, edge cases, workflow transitions and tool failures, along with crisp evaluation criteria."},contextInjection:"The user wants to create test cases for their agent. Walk them through: 1) Start with the happy path — the most common successful conversation flow, 2) Add edge cases: caller says something off-topic, asks to speak to a human, gives invalid input, hangs up mid-flow, 3) For workflow agents: test each node transition — does the agent move to the right node at the right time? Does it get stuck?, 4) For tool-dependent agents: test tool success, tool failure, and tool timeout scenarios, 5) Define clear evaluation criteria — use the 'prompt' type with specific questions like 'Did the agent collect all required fields?' rather than vague goals like 'Was the call successful?', 6) For telephony: test with realistic audio conditions — background noise, interruptions, garbled speech, 7) Set up a regular testing cadence after making configuration changes.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["create_test","list_tests"]},{id:"agent-config:tests:debug-failures",title:{id:"JRvFM6",message:"Why are my tests failing?"},userMessage:{id:"oCqlt4",message:"Help me debug my failing tests step by step. Look at where the agent diverged, whether the failure is in content, tools or flow, and whether evaluation criteria are too strict."},contextInjection:"The user has tests that are failing. Debug step by step: 1) Review the test transcript to see exactly where the agent diverged from expectations, 2) Check if the failure is in the agent's response content, tool usage, or conversation flow, 3) For workflow agents: check if the agent transitioned to the wrong node — look for edge conditions that are too vague (e.g., 'Customer is identified' should be 'client_lookup tool returned a matching record'), 4) Check if evaluation criteria are too strict — criteria run post-call via separate LLM calls, so overly specific criteria may fail on valid conversations, 5) If the agent skips workflow steps: check for tool scoping issues (set tool_ids: [] at base, use additional_tool_ids per node) and add boundary instructions to each node, 6) Fix the root cause and re-run to verify.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["list_tests","get_test","list_test_runs","get_test_suite_summary","get_test_suite_failures","get_test_run_detail","update_test","run_tests"]}],nJ=[{id:"agent-config:workflow:getting-started",title:{id:"aT0lCz",message:"Ready to go from start node to full workflow?"},contextInjection:"",video:nB.AGENT_WORKFLOW_VIDEO_SERIES,priority:nz.High,condition:e=>!!e.isWorkflowEmpty},{id:"agent-config:workflow:create",title:{id:"xK8-Sx",message:"How should I structure my workflow?"},userMessage:{id:"VaieQ1",message:"Help me design a conversation workflow for my agent. Walk me through identifying split points, choosing data vs scoping vs judgment routing, edge conventions and tool scoping."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user wants to create a workflow. Walk them through the architecture framework: 1) Map out the conversation flow — identify the major phases and decision points, 2) Assess each potential split point: is it DATA-driven (tool result → deterministic routing), SCOPING (dangerous tool must be restricted), or JUDGMENT (LLM decides)? Default to fewer nodes — only split when the risk assessment justifies it, 3) For DATA routing: use a tool node with expression edges (most reliable). For SCOPING: use node boundaries with additional_tool_ids. For JUDGMENT: prefer keeping it in prompt-level phases unless wrong-routing cost is HIGH, 4) Create ONE edge per node pair — use forward_condition for A→B and backward_condition for B→A on the SAME edge. Two edges between the same nodes causes a 'Duplicate edge found' deployment error, 5) Write tight edge conditions referencing tool results and verbal confirmations, not vague states ('client_lookup returned a match' not 'Customer is identified'), 6) Set tool_ids: [] on the base agent and assign tools per node via additional_tool_ids, 7) Test each transition path end-to-end.",priority:nz.High},{id:"agent-config:workflow:debug",title:{id:"Un0SPj",message:"Why is my workflow misbehaving?"},userMessage:{id:"XedatJ",message:"Help me debug my workflow. Walk me through the common failure modes (stuck in node, transitioning too early, dead air, repeated questions, duplicate edges) and pinpoint which I'm hitting."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user's workflow isn't behaving as expected. Debug using the known failure modes: 1) Agent stays stuck in one node: the LLM never called the notify_condition tool — check if the edge condition is vague or doesn't match the actual conversation flow; tighten it with tool result references, 2) Agent transitions too early: the platform's meta-prompt encourages speculative transitions — add boundary instructions to the node prompt ('Do NOT proceed until X is confirmed') and make edge conditions more specific, 3) Dead air during transitions: each node transition is a full LLM brain teardown + rebuild — reduce unnecessary nodes by merging sequential phases into single nodes with internal phase labels (# Phase 1, # Phase 2), 4) Agent repeats questions after transition: conversation history carries over but the new node may re-ask; add 'Review the conversation history — do not re-collect information already provided' to node prompts, 5) Duplicate edge error on deploy: ensure only ONE edge exists between any two nodes — use backward_condition for the return direction, 6) Edge conditions overlap: if multiple outgoing edges have similar conditions, the LLM may fire the wrong one — make conditions mutually exclusive.",priority:nz.High},{id:"agent-config:workflow:prevent-skipping",title:{id:"veRv6_",message:"Why is my agent skipping workflow steps?"},userMessage:{id:"P1y8jC",message:"Help me stop my agent from skipping workflow steps or calling tools from the wrong phase. Cover tool scoping, boundary instructions, tighter edge conditions and root prompt cleanup."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user's agent is skipping workflow steps or calling tools from the wrong phase. This is the most common workflow issue. Walk them through all three defenses: 1) TOOL SCOPING: Set tool_ids: [] in the base agent config — this removes all tools globally. Then assign tools to specific nodes via additional_tool_ids. This way cancel_appointment is only available in the cancellation node, not everywhere, 2) BOUNDARY INSTRUCTIONS: Add a # Boundaries section to every node prompt listing what it must NOT do: 'Do NOT discuss pricing (that's handled in the sales node)', 'Do NOT call any tools other than client_lookup', 'Do NOT ask about scheduling until authentication is complete', 3) TIGHT EDGE CONDITIONS: Replace vague conditions with observable evidence: instead of 'Authentication complete' write 'client_lookup tool returned a matching record AND caller verbally confirmed their name and date of birth', 4) ROOT PROMPT CLEANUP: Remove flow descriptions from the root/base prompt ('Step 1: Greet, Step 2: Verify...'). The root prompt should only have personality, tone, and guardrails — flow descriptions give the LLM a roadmap to skip ahead. Node-specific flow goes in additional_prompt.",priority:nz.High},{id:"agent-config:workflow:dynamic-variables",title:{id:"4URlcN",message:"How do dynamic variables work in workflows?"},userMessage:{id:"2CSueF",message:"Explain how dynamic variables work in workflows: how they're set, how to update them mid-conversation, how they flow across nodes and how to use them in edge conditions and the first message."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user wants to understand how dynamic variables work within workflows. Walk them through: 1) Dynamic variables are set at conversation start (via SDK, pre-call webhook, or batch calling CSV) and persist throughout the conversation. They populate {{variable_name}} placeholders in prompts and tool parameters, 2) System variables are automatically available: {{system__time}}, {{system__call_duration_secs}}, {{system__call_id}}, {{system__caller_id}} — reference them in any node prompt, 3) To UPDATE a dynamic variable during a conversation: use the update_state system tool (UpdateStateToolConfig). This executes internally with no external request — no latency penalty. Define state updates with expression-based assignments (e.g., set custom_var to a new value based on a tool result), 4) Variables flow across node transitions — if a tool in node A sets {{customer_name}}, node B's prompt can reference it. But variables only work with external data (pre-call webhook, SDK, tool responses) — NOT with data the agent extracts from conversation alone, 5) For edge conditions: reference dynamic variables in expression edges for deterministic routing (e.g., 'if {{account_type}} == premium → route to premium support node'), 6) For batch calling: CSV columns map directly to dynamic variables — column 'first_name' becomes {{first_name}} in the prompt. Define dynamic_variable_placeholders with sensible defaults for missing values, 7) For the first_message: if using dynamic variables in the greeting, use the pre-call webhook to fetch values BEFORE the call connects — otherwise the variable won't be populated when the first message plays, 8) Tip: inject the variable value explicitly in the system prompt ('The customer's name in this conversation is {{customer_name}}') rather than relying on the LLM to evaluate conditional statements.",priority:nz.High},{id:"agent-config:workflow:fix-edges",title:{id:"S8rHmT",message:"Why are my edges erroring on deploy?"},userMessage:{id:"vK4jZ0",message:"Help me fix edge-related deployment errors and runtime transition failures (duplicate edges, self-loops, terminal node edges, infinite backward conditions, vague conditions)."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user has edge-related deployment errors or runtime transition failures. Walk them through common fixes: 1) 'Duplicate edge found': they have two edges between the same pair of nodes. Merge into ONE edge — use forward_condition for the A→B direction and backward_condition for B→A. Name edges without direction (edge-greeting-billing not edge-greeting-to-billing), 2) 'An edge cannot connect to itself': remove the self-loop edge and put the looping behavior in the node's prompt instead ('Continue answering questions until they indicate they're done'), 3) 'It's not possible to transition from Phone transfer': phone_number and end nodes are TERMINAL — remove outgoing edges. For transfer fallback, use the transfer_to_number system tool instead of a phone_number node, 4) Unconditional backward_condition: this creates an infinite loop — change it to an LLM condition with a specific trigger, 5) Multiple LLM conditions in an expression: combine into a single LLM condition using 'OR' ('Caller asking about billing OR payments'), 6) Edge condition too vague and firing unpredictably: reference specific tool results and verbal confirmations.",priority:nz.Medium}],nX=[{id:"agent-config:analysis:setup-criteria",title:{id:"Z_W3Nr",message:"How do I write good evaluation criteria?"},userMessage:{id:"UMFiG5",message:"Help me define evaluation criteria for post-call analysis. Cover writing them as questions, being specific about success/failure, handling early termination and grouping checks."},contextInjection:"The user wants to define evaluation criteria for post-call analysis. Walk them through best practices: 1) Write criteria as evaluation questions, not agent instructions — 'Evaluate whether the agent collected all required fields' not 'The agent should collect all fields', 2) Be specific about success/failure: 'Mark SUCCESS only if the agent read the disclosure starting with [exact phrase]' not 'Did the agent read the disclosure?', 3) Always include UNKNOWN guidance for early termination: 'If the call ended before reaching this phase, mark UNKNOWN rather than FAILURE', 4) Keep to 3-5 criteria covering: compliance (were required disclosures read?), completeness (were all data fields collected?), accuracy (were facts stated correctly?), and boundaries (did the agent stay in scope?), 5) Group related checks: instead of 4 separate disclosure criteria, combine into one: 'Evaluate whether ALL of the following were read: (1)..., (2)..., (3)...', 6) Each criterion is a separate LLM call — conversation_goal_prompt max is 2000 characters, so be concise but specific.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-config:analysis:setup-data-collection",title:{id:"N33UFn",message:"How do I extract structured data from calls?"},userMessage:{id:"oVOSCl",message:"Help me set up post-call data collection. Cover field types, when to use enums, why fields can't reference each other, and designing for null when calls end early."},contextInjection:"The user wants to extract structured data from conversations automatically. Walk them through the data_collection configuration: 1) Define fields with clear extraction instructions in the description — the LLM uses description as its prompt ('The caller's full legal name as confirmed during the call' not just 'Customer name'), 2) Choose the right type: boolean for yes/no facts ('Did the caller authorize the inquiry?'), string with enum for categories ('call_outcome' with enum: ['completed', 'declined', 'transferred']), integer/number for amounts ('monthly_income in dollars'), 3) Use enum liberally — even large enums (50+ values) give the extraction LLM stronger guidance than descriptions alone, 4) Each field is extracted independently in a separate LLM call — never reference other fields in descriptions ('Only if coverage_active is yes' won't work because the extraction for this field doesn't see coverage_active's result), 5) Never request comma-separated multi-value responses — the platform only supports scalar types. Split into a primary enum field + secondary free-text field, 6) Keep descriptions under ~500 characters — long descriptions dilute the extraction instruction, 7) Design for null: if the caller hangs up early, most fields will be null — that's expected, not an error.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config"]}],nZ=[{id:"agent-config:coaching:what-is-coaching",title:{id:"Hg1Ybn",message:"What is agent coaching?"},userMessage:{id:"U2gYOO",message:"Explain what agent coaching does and how proposals are generated. Help me understand the workflow from conversation to applied improvements."},contextInjection:"The user is viewing the Coaching tab and wants to understand how agent coaching works. Explain: 1) Coaching is an AI-assisted way to improve your agent by reviewing real conversations with a virtual coach, 2) It is interactive, not automatic: you open a past conversation under Analysis > Conversation history and click the coaching button next to an agent message to start a coaching chat (you can also trigger coaching from an integration that has the Agent Coaching option enabled), 3) In that chat you discuss the interaction with the virtual coach, and the coach proposes concrete improvements as proposals, 4) Proposals appear in this table with types: 'Edit system prompt' (agent_prompt_change), 'Create/Edit/Delete memory' (memory_entry_create/update/delete), or 'Edit procedure' (procedure_update), 5) Each proposal shows the conversation it came from, the proposed change, and its status (pending, approved, or rejected), 6) You review each proposal and approve or reject it; approving applies the change to your agent configuration and rejecting discards it, 7) Coaching is enabled via the 'Enable coaching' toggle in the Advanced settings tab, and you must publish the agent for the change to take effect, 8) The feature is gated behind the convai_agent_coaching feature flag.",priority:nz.High},{id:"agent-config:coaching:review-proposals",title:{id:"SrGkBn",message:"How do I review and apply coaching proposals?"},userMessage:{id:"vi_dj_",message:"Walk me through how to review coaching proposals effectively. Cover filtering, understanding proposal types, evaluating changes, and when to approve vs reject."},contextInjection:"The user wants to learn how to review and manage coaching proposals. Walk them through: 1) Use the Status filter to focus on 'pending' proposals that need review, 2) Use the Type filter to review similar proposals together (e.g. all 'Edit system prompt' proposals), 3) Click a proposal to see the full details including the conversation that triggered it and the proposed change, 4) For system prompt changes (agent_prompt_change): compare the before/after diff carefully because small wording changes can significantly affect agent behavior, 5) For memory changes (memory_entry_create/update/delete): verify the information is accurate and doesn't duplicate existing content, 6) For procedure updates: ensure the workflow logic remains correct, 7) Approve proposals that improve the agent based on real conversation issues; approving applies the change to your agent configuration, 8) Reject proposals that are too narrow (only help one edge case), incorrect, or contradict your agent's core behavior, 9) Consider the conversation context: was this a representative interaction or an unusual edge case?",priority:nz.High},{id:"agent-config:coaching:no-proposals",title:{id:"wnW_iH",message:"Why don't I have any coaching proposals?"},userMessage:{id:"Fraicp",message:"Help me understand why no proposals are appearing. Check if coaching is enabled, how proposals are created, and if the feature is working correctly."},contextInjection:"The user has no coaching proposals and wants to understand why. The most common reason is that proposals are not generated automatically: they are only created when you actively coach the agent. Help them troubleshoot: 1) Proposals come from coaching chats. Open a past conversation under Analysis > Conversation history, click the coaching button next to an agent message, and discuss the interaction with the virtual coach. The coach proposes improvements during that chat, and they then appear in this table. If you haven't started a coaching chat yet, there will be no proposals, 2) Check that coaching is enabled: go to the Advanced settings tab (or Settings > Advanced in agent-centric view), verify 'Enable coaching' is toggled on, and publish the agent so the change takes effect, 3) Check the filters: if Status is set to 'approved' or 'rejected' and nothing matches, switch to 'pending' or clear the filters, 4) The feature requires the convai_agent_coaching feature flag, so verify your workspace has access, 5) You can also generate proposals by setting up an integration trigger with the Agent Coaching option enabled.",priority:nz.Medium}],n0=[{id:"agent-config:overview:improve-metrics",title:{id:"Sov3qJ",message:"How do I improve my agent's metrics?"},userMessage:{id:"zrHGya",message:"Help me improve my agent's performance metrics. Walk me through metric-specific fixes for latency, success rate, drop-off and user experience, and recommend where to start."},contextInjection:"The user wants to improve their agent's performance metrics. Walk them through metric-specific fixes: 1) HIGH LATENCY: switch TTS to eleven_flash_v2 (English) or eleven_flash_v2_5 (multilingual), choose a faster LLM from the mini tier (gpt-4o-mini at ~736ms or gpt-4.1-mini at ~834ms), set cascade_timeout to 3s for telephony, reduce node count in workflows (each transition = dead air), 2) LOW SUCCESS RATE: review failing conversations in the history tab, check if edge conditions are too vague causing wrong transitions, improve the system prompt with clearer boundaries, add missing knowledge base content, check evaluation criteria aren't too strict, 3) HIGH DROP-OFF: review where callers hang up — is there dead air during transitions? Is the agent asking too many questions? Are tool calls timing out?, 4) POOR USER EXPERIENCE: adjust turn-taking settings (lower eagerness for patient callers), improve the first_message to set expectations, add skip_turn for hold/silence handling on telephony, 5) Set up A/B tests using branches to measure improvement before rolling out changes.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["get_agent_config","list_conversations","update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-config:overview:open-telemetry-webhook",title:{id:"EbZlLg",message:"How do I set up OpenTelemetry traces?"},userMessage:{id:"a--l8i",message:"I want to export conversations as OpenTelemetry traces for my observability stack. Walk me through setting up a post-call webhook with OTLP payloads."},contextInjection:"When the user asks to export conversations as OpenTelemetry traces, send OTLP payloads to an observability stack, or configure post-call webhooks for tracing.",priority:nz.Medium}],n1=[{id:"agent-config:insights:topic-analyze",title:{id:"o0BbsK",message:"Analyse conversations in this topic"},userMessage:{id:"An7G4z",message:"Read through conversations in this topic and identify patterns: common questions, recurring issues, sentiment trends, and opportunities for improvement."},contextInjection:"The user is viewing a specific topic in the topics visualization and wants to analyse conversations within that topic. Help them by: 1) Summarize what this topic is about based on the topic label and description, 2) Identify common caller intents within this topic — what are people actually asking for?, 3) Look for recurring issues: are there questions the agent consistently can't answer? Are callers getting frustrated?, 4) Identify sentiment patterns: are conversations in this topic generally positive, negative, or mixed?, 5) Check resolution rates: are conversations in this topic ending successfully or failing?, 6) Recommend specific improvements: knowledge base content to add, prompt adjustments, or workflow changes that would help this topic.",priority:nz.High,condition:e=>!!e.viewedTopic,recommendedTools:["list_conversations","get_conversation_detail"]},{id:"agent-config:insights:topic-improve",title:{id:"X5LTC-",message:"Improve agent handling of this topic"},userMessage:{id:"tGv9CD",message:"Help me improve how my agent handles this topic. Analyse the conversations, identify gaps, and recommend specific changes to prompts, knowledge base, or workflows."},contextInjection:"The user is viewing a specific topic and wants to improve how their agent handles it. Walk them through: 1) Review sample conversations from this topic to understand current agent behavior, 2) Identify knowledge gaps: are there questions the agent can't answer? Add missing knowledge base content, 3) Check prompt coverage: does the system prompt mention this topic? If not, add relevant instructions, 4) For workflow agents: is there a dedicated node for this topic? Should there be?, 5) Review tone and response quality: is the agent responding appropriately for this topic's nature?, 6) Set up test cases specifically for this topic to prevent regressions, 7) Consider whether this topic should trigger specific tools or transfers.",priority:nz.High,condition:e=>!!e.viewedTopic,recommendedTools:["list_knowledge_base","create_document","update_agent_config","create_test"]},{id:"agent-config:insights:topic-read-conversations",title:{id:"cvi0eo",message:"Read conversations in this topic"},userMessage:{id:"z8oGDM",message:"Show me a sample of conversations from this topic. I want to understand what callers are actually saying and how my agent is responding."},contextInjection:"The user wants to read actual conversations from the topic they're viewing. Help them by: 1) Retrieve recent conversations that belong to this topic, 2) For each conversation, highlight: the main caller intent, how the agent responded, whether the call was successful, and any interesting moments, 3) Look for patterns across conversations: common openers, recurring questions, typical conversation flow, 4) Identify outliers: conversations that went unusually well or poorly compared to the average, 5) Summarize findings in a way that helps the user understand what's really happening in this topic.",priority:nz.Medium,condition:e=>!!e.viewedTopic,recommendedTools:["list_conversations","get_conversation_detail"]},{id:"agent-config:insights:global-topic-overview",title:{id:"nQ_Nmq",message:"What are my top conversation topics?"},userMessage:{id:"LK6j3n",message:"Analyse my agent's trending topics. What are the most common things callers ask about, which topics have the most issues, and what should I prioritize improving?"},contextInjection:"The user is viewing the global topics visualization and wants to understand their agent's conversation topics. Help them by: 1) Review the topic distribution — which topics have the highest conversation volume?, 2) Identify problem topics: which topics have low success rates or high drop-off?, 3) Look for unexpected topics: are callers asking about things you didn't anticipate?, 4) Check topic coverage: are there important topics missing from your knowledge base or prompt?, 5) Prioritize improvements: recommend which topics to focus on based on volume × impact, 6) Consider topic relationships: are some topics overlapping or causing confusion?",priority:nz.High,condition:e=>!e.viewedTopic&&!!e.isTopicsFullscreen,recommendedTools:["list_conversations","get_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-config:insights:global-knowledge-gaps",title:{id:"oYviex",message:"What knowledge gaps do I have?"},userMessage:{id:"t3b_W8",message:"Analyse my topics to find knowledge gaps. Which topics have the most unanswered questions, and what content should I add to my knowledge base?"},contextInjection:"The user wants to identify knowledge gaps across their topics. Help them by: 1) Review topics with high conversation volume but low success rates — these likely have knowledge gaps, 2) Look at topic descriptions to understand what questions callers are asking, 3) Cross-reference with the knowledge base: are these topics covered? Is the content accurate and complete?, 4) Identify commonly asked questions within each topic that the agent struggles to answer, 5) Recommend specific knowledge base articles to create or update, 6) Prioritize by impact: focus on high-volume topics first, 7) Consider whether some gaps need prompt changes rather than knowledge base additions.",priority:nz.High,condition:e=>!e.viewedTopic&&!!e.isTopicsFullscreen,recommendedTools:["list_knowledge_base","create_document","list_conversations"]},{id:"agent-config:insights:understand-topics",title:{id:"AY0MgK",message:"How do topics work?"},userMessage:{id:"fnwCIM",message:"Explain how the topics visualization works. How are topics identified, how should I interpret the visualization, and how can I use this to improve my agent?"},contextInjection:"The user wants to understand the topics feature. Explain: 1) Topics are automatically identified by analyzing conversation content using ML clustering — no manual tagging required, 2) Topic size in the visualization reflects conversation volume — larger circles = more conversations about that topic, 3) Subtopics (nested circles) show related but distinct conversation themes within a parent topic, 4) Clicking a topic shows its conversation history so you can see what callers are actually saying, 5) Use topics to: identify what callers ask about most, find knowledge gaps, discover unexpected use cases, prioritize improvements, 6) Topics update as new conversations come in — check back regularly to spot emerging trends, 7) The search bar (in fullscreen) helps find specific topics quickly.",priority:nz.Medium,condition:e=>!e.viewedTopic}],n5=[{id:"agent-analytics:general:why-slow",title:{id:"UbsKl4",message:"Why is this agent slow?"},userMessage:{id:"a7rX9S",message:"Walk me through this agent's latency numbers. Help me find the biggest contributor (TTS, LLM, cascade, prompt length, workflow nodes) and what to change first."},contextInjection:"The user is looking at one specific agent's analytics and wants to understand why latency is high. Walk them through the dashboard: 1) Look at the end-to-end latency breakdown if available — separate TTS, LLM, ASR, and workflow transition time, 2) If LLM latency dominates: check which model is configured (heavy models like gpt-4o, claude-sonnet add hundreds of ms vs gpt-4o-mini/gemini-2.5-flash), check cascade_timeout (should be 3s on telephony so fallback fires fast), 3) If TTS latency dominates: switch to eleven_flash_v2 (English) or eleven_flash_v2_5 (multilingual) — V3 is slower, 4) If there's dead air mid-conversation: that's workflow transitions — each node hop is a full LLM teardown/rebuild. Merge sequential phases into single nodes with internal phase labels, 5) Long system prompts increase first-token latency — move detailed instructions into node-level additional_prompt where they only load when needed, 6) Compare against the agent's branches if A/B testing — one branch may already be faster.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["get_agent_config","get_workflow","update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-analytics:general:why-failing",title:{id:"aYblki",message:"Why is this agent failing calls?"},userMessage:{id:"9X-Jhy",message:"Help me read this agent's success metrics. Identify what's driving failures — drop-off, low success rate, evaluation criteria — and recommend the highest-impact fix."},contextInjection:"The user is looking at one specific agent's analytics and wants to understand why call_successful rate is low or drop-off is high. Walk them through: 1) Distinguish success rate (post-call evaluator verdict) from drop-off (caller hung up early) — they have different root causes, 2) For low success rate: open a few failing conversations in the history tab — is the agent giving wrong answers (knowledge base gap), getting stuck in the wrong workflow node (vague edge condition), or failing tool calls (auth/timeout)? Also verify evaluation criteria aren't unrealistically strict — if calls end early, criteria should mark UNKNOWN, not FAILURE, 3) For high drop-off: look at where conversations end — long dead air during transitions (reduce node count), repetitive questions (add 'review conversation history' to node prompts), or tool timeouts mid-flow (raise response_timeout_secs, simplify request bodies), 4) Compare this agent's metrics against your other agents — is the system prompt structured (Personality/Tone/Guardrails sections) or a wall of text?, 5) If this agent has branches: compare per-branch metrics before rolling out changes.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["list_conversations","get_conversation_detail","list_tests","update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-analytics:general:read-dashboard",title:{id:"uUhUgZ",message:"How do I read this dashboard?"},userMessage:{id:"nurpai",message:"Walk me through what each chart on this analytics page is actually measuring, what 'good' looks like for each, and which numbers I should care about first."},contextInjection:"The user wants help interpreting the per-agent analytics dashboard. Walk them through the main charts: 1) Call count / minutes used — raw volume; useful for cost sanity checks and noticing traffic shifts, 2) call_successful rate — set per-conversation by the evaluation criteria; if you haven't configured criteria, this number is meaningless. Configure 3-5 criteria in the Analysis tab first, 3) Latency metrics — typically broken down by stage (LLM, TTS, ASR). Telephony targets: <1s end-to-end response. Anything >1.5s feels sluggish, 4) Drop-off / hangup rate — measures how often callers leave mid-conversation. High drop-off near the start usually means poor first_message or auth friction; mid-call drop-off usually means dead air or repeated questions, 5) Date range and branch filters: use these to compare before/after a config change or to compare A/B test branches, 6) Empty charts (---): no data has been collected for the selected range — extend the range or wait for traffic.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["navigate_agent_subpage"]},{id:"agent-analytics:general:compare-branches",title:{id:"6t7Qnx",message:"How do I compare branches in analytics?"},userMessage:{id:"EjMxZz",message:"Help me compare branches on this analytics page. Walk me through filtering by branch, what to look for in head-to-head metrics, and when it's safe to roll out a winning branch."},contextInjection:"The user wants to compare branches using the analytics dashboard. Walk them through: 1) Use the branch filter at the top of the dashboard to add multiple branches side by side — the views support a 'Branch' grouping, 2) Compare like-for-like over a window where both branches received meaningful traffic (at least a few hundred conversations per branch for stable averages), 3) Key metrics to compare: success rate, latency, drop-off, and evaluation criteria pass-rates — not just one number in isolation, 4) Watch for confounders: did one branch only get traffic at night? Did one branch handle a different user segment? Pick a date range where the traffic split was stable, 5) Statistical reality check: differences <5% on small samples are noise. Wait for more data or be ready for the metric to regress when you raise the traffic share, 6) Once a branch wins decisively, ramp gradually (10% → 50% → 100%) and keep watching analytics during ramp — issues sometimes only appear at higher traffic.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["list_branches","set_traffic_split","navigate_agent_subpage"]}],n2=[{id:"agent-analytics:evaluation:improve-success",title:{id:"VCn-fv",message:"How do I lift this agent's success rate?"},userMessage:{id:"B3KiGn",message:"Help me improve this agent's success rate. Identify its most-failed evaluation criteria, look for patterns in its failing conversations, and recommend the highest-impact fixes."},contextInjection:"The user is viewing this agent's evaluation analytics and wants to improve its success rate. Walk them through: 1) Identify which of this agent's evaluation criteria fail most often — focus fixes on the most common failure, 2) Review this agent's failing conversations to find patterns — concentrated on specific times, channels, or user types?, 3) If this agent uses a workflow: check whether failures correlate with specific node transitions — vague edge conditions cause skipped or repeated steps, 4) Check whether the criteria are too strict or ambiguous — criteria that fail on valid conversations waste attention, 5) For each common failure, trace to the root cause (prompt wording, tool config, knowledge base gap) and fix it on this agent, then re-run its tests to verify.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["list_conversations","get_conversation_detail","update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-analytics:evaluation:setup-criteria",title:{id:"aGWMuq",message:"How do I write good evaluation criteria for this agent?"},userMessage:{id:"lifFeJ",message:"Help me define evaluation criteria for this agent's post-call analysis. Cover writing them as questions, being specific about success/failure, handling early termination and grouping checks."},contextInjection:"The user wants to define evaluation criteria for this agent's post-call analysis. Tailor suggestions to what this agent actually does. Walk them through: 1) Write criteria as evaluation questions, not agent instructions — 'Evaluate whether the agent collected all required fields' not 'The agent should collect all fields', 2) Be specific about success/failure: 'Mark SUCCESS only if the agent read the disclosure starting with [exact phrase]' not 'Did the agent read the disclosure?', 3) Always include UNKNOWN guidance for early termination: 'If the call ended before reaching this phase, mark UNKNOWN rather than FAILURE', 4) Keep to 3-5 criteria covering: compliance, completeness, accuracy, and boundaries — matched to this agent's actual flow, 5) Each criterion is a separate LLM call — keep criteria concise but specific.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config"]}],n3=[{id:"agent-analytics:data-collection:setup",title:{id:"no6h1R",message:"How do I extract structured data from this agent's calls?"},userMessage:{id:"6qq1tr",message:"Help me set up post-call data collection for this agent. Cover field types, when to use enums, why fields can't reference each other, and designing for null when calls end early."},contextInjection:"The user wants to extract structured data from this agent's conversations. Walk them through the data_collection configuration: 1) Define fields with clear extraction instructions in the description, 2) Choose the right type: boolean for yes/no, string with enum for categories, integer/number for amounts, 3) Use enum liberally — even large enums give stronger guidance than descriptions alone, 4) Each field is extracted independently — never reference other fields in descriptions, 5) Never request comma-separated multi-value responses — the platform only supports scalar types, 6) Design for null: if the caller hangs up early, most fields will be null — that's expected. Suggest fields that match the data this agent's flow actually gathers.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-analytics:data-collection:fix-extraction",title:{id:"2nZNQh",message:"Why isn't this agent's data extraction working?"},userMessage:{id:"9nz8Mf",message:"Help me debug this agent's data collection fields that aren't extracting. Check field descriptions, types, enum coverage, and whether the target data actually appears in its transcripts."},contextInjection:"This agent's data collection fields aren't extracting correctly. Debug step by step: 1) Check if the field description clearly tells the extraction LLM what to look for — vague descriptions produce unreliable results, 2) Verify the field type matches the expected data — using 'string' for a yes/no field produces 'yes'/'no' strings instead of true/false booleans, 3) For enum fields: check that the enum values cover all realistic possibilities — missing values cause null results, 4) Review this agent's conversation transcripts to see if the target data actually appears — null extraction is expected when the information was never discussed, 5) Keep descriptions under ~500 characters — long descriptions dilute the instruction, 6) Each field is independent — the extraction for field A cannot see field B's result.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["list_conversations","get_conversation_detail","update_agent_config"]}],n8=[{id:"agent-analytics:tools:reduce-failures",title:{id:"JO-IPW",message:"Why are this agent's tools failing?"},userMessage:{id:"pzbGVo",message:"Help me reduce this agent's tool call failures. Identify which of its tools fail most and walk me through fixing webhook errors, timeouts, wrong calls, missing calls and bad parameters."},contextInjection:"The user is looking at this agent's tool analytics and wants to reduce failure rates. Walk them through: 1) Identify which of this agent's tools fail most — a single tool or widespread?, 2) For webhook tool failures: check the endpoint URL accessibility, authentication headers (expired keys/tokens?), and request body format, 3) For timeout failures: increase response_timeout_secs (default 20s, max 120s) if the API is slow, 4) For wrong tool calls: improve the tool description — the LLM uses it to decide when to call the tool, 5) For missing tool calls: check tool scoping — is the tool available in the right workflow node of this agent?, 6) For tools called with wrong parameters: check parameter descriptions and ensure they clearly describe expected values.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["list_tools","get_conversation_detail","update_tool"]},{id:"agent-analytics:tools:optimize-latency",title:{id:"yCfZoa",message:"Why are this agent's tool calls slow?"},userMessage:{id:"O2Vjq_",message:"Help me reduce this agent's tool call latency. Identify its slowest tools, decide whether to migrate MCP tools to webhooks, and look for ways to merge sequential tool chains."},contextInjection:"The user wants to reduce this agent's tool call latency. Walk them through: 1) Check response times for each of this agent's tools — which are slowest?, 2) For slow webhook tools: optimize the downstream API or increase server capacity, 3) For MCP tools: MCP adds protocol overhead — for latency-sensitive voice agents, consider migrating critical tools to webhook format, 4) Reduce tool call chains — if the agent calls tool A, then B, then C sequentially, latency compounds. Consider merging into a single endpoint, 5) Set response_timeout_secs appropriately — too high wastes time on dead endpoints, too low causes false timeouts.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["list_tools","update_tool"]}],n4=[{id:"agent-analytics:llms:compare-models",title:{id:"Ux5wnx",message:"Is this agent on the right LLM?"},userMessage:{id:"kdfkeN",message:"Help me read this agent's LLM analytics: its latency, cascade fallback rate, error rate and provider diversity, and recommend whether to change its primary or backups."},contextInjection:"The user is viewing this agent's LLM analytics and wants to know if its model choice is right. Walk them through: 1) Compare this agent's latency against the model tiers — gpt-4o-mini (~736ms) and gpt-4.1-mini (~834ms) are fastest, gemini-2.5-flash (~1.05s) is best value, claude-sonnet-4.6 (~937ms) is best for complex reasoning, 2) Check this agent's cascade fallback rate — if the primary frequently times out, the cascade fires two models (double cost), 3) For telephony: 3s cascade timeout is recommended — any model slower than that triggers fallbacks, 4) Check error rates — 429 rate limiting means hitting provider quotas, 5) Use cross-provider diversity in the cascade (e.g., primary Gemini, backup OpenAI, backup Anthropic) to avoid correlated failures. Recommend a concrete change to this agent's cascade.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-analytics:llms:reduce-costs",title:{id:"nP8cUe",message:"How do I cut this agent's LLM cost?"},userMessage:{id:"CqQQQr",message:"Help me reduce this agent's LLM spend. Tell me whether I can downgrade its model, shorten its prompt or cut workflow nodes without hurting quality."},contextInjection:"The user wants to reduce this agent's LLM cost. Walk them through: 1) Check this agent's model and cost — gpt-4o is ~$0.037/min while gemini-2.5-flash is ~$0.002/min, 2) If this agent does simple work, downgrade to a mini model — a basic FAQ agent doesn't need gpt-4o, 3) Shorten this agent's system prompt — every token is processed on every turn, 4) If it uses a workflow, reduce node count — fewer cascade restarts means less LLM usage, 5) Check this agent's cascade timeout — frequent fallbacks mean paying for two models per turn. Recommend the specific change with the best cost/quality tradeoff for this agent.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config"]}],n6=[{id:"agent-analytics:kb:improve-retrieval",title:{id:"mho3Og",message:"How do I improve this agent's KB retrieval?"},userMessage:{id:"vJ1vYm",message:"Help me improve retrieval for this agent's knowledge base based on its analytics: question-style headings, chunk size, deduping, embedding model choice and tuning max_vector_distance."},contextInjection:"The user is viewing this agent's knowledge base analytics and wants to improve retrieval. Walk them through: 1) Use question-style headings in documents — retrieval queries are rewritten as questions, so Q-style headings dramatically increase embedding similarity, 2) Keep articles to 1.5-3 KB for optimal chunking, 3) Minimize duplicate content across this agent's documents — duplicates waste retrieval slots, 4) Remove YAML front matter — it becomes noise in the first chunk, 5) Adjust max_vector_distance (default 0.6) — lower is stricter, higher retrieves more but less relevant, 6) For non-English content: use multilingual_e5_large_instruct embedding model.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["list_knowledge_base","update_document"]},{id:"agent-analytics:kb:add-content",title:{id:"g09L_J",message:"What content is missing from this agent's KB?"},userMessage:{id:"mvmAec",message:"Help me figure out what knowledge base content this agent is missing. Look at its conversation history for unanswered questions and recommend articles to write in Q&A format."},contextInjection:"The user wants to know what knowledge base content this agent is missing. Walk them through: 1) Review this agent's conversation history for questions it couldn't answer — these are the highest-priority content gaps, 2) Structure each new article around a single question or topic, 3) Write in Q&A format with question-style headings, 4) Include the exact phrasing this agent's callers actually use — not just internal terminology, 5) Keep articles self-contained — cross-references don't work since the LLM only sees retrieved chunks, 6) Attach new documents to this agent (or per-node via additional_knowledge_base) so they're actually retrieved.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["list_conversations","get_conversation_detail","list_knowledge_base"]}],n7=[{id:"agent-analytics:advanced:understand-metrics",title:{id:"iK1MP5",message:"What do this agent's metrics mean?"},userMessage:{id:"_zloRy",message:"Walk me through what each metric on this agent's analytics view means: latency, success rate, call duration, drop-off and tool call success, and what good numbers look like."},contextInjection:"The user is viewing this agent's conversation-level analytics and wants to understand the metrics. Walk them through: 1) Average latency: time from user finishing speaking to agent starting to respond — includes ASR, LLM, and TTS. Below 1.5s is good for telephony, 2) Success rate: percentage of this agent's conversations where all evaluation criteria passed — requires criteria to be configured, 3) Call duration: average conversation length — compare against this agent's expected flow duration to spot stuck agents or premature hangups, 4) Drop-off rate: where users abandon — high drop-off during transitions indicates dead air from too many workflow nodes, 5) Tool call success rate: percentage of this agent's tool calls returning without errors — low rates indicate configuration issues, 6) Filter by date range to isolate trends for this agent.",priority:nz.High},{id:"agent-analytics:advanced:export-data",title:{id:"f_kKF9",message:"How do I export this agent's conversation data?"},userMessage:{id:"n5uTfF",message:"Help me access this agent's conversation data programmatically: the API endpoints, filtering by this agent, pagination, and when to use post-call webhooks instead of polling."},contextInjection:"The user wants to export or access this agent's conversation data programmatically. Filter by this agent's agent_id. Walk them through: 1) Use the API to fetch this agent's conversations: GET /v1/convai/conversations?agent_id=<this agent> for listing, GET /v1/convai/conversations/{id} for details, 2) Paginate with cursor-based pagination for large datasets, 3) Filter by date range and call result to narrow results, 4) Each conversation includes: transcript, tool calls, data collection results, evaluation scores, and metadata, 5) For bulk analysis: set up a post-call webhook (on this agent's Security tab or workspace default) to receive conversation data in real-time as calls complete — more efficient than polling, 6) Data retention follows the plan's retention period.",priority:nz.Medium}],n9=[{id:"agent-history:find-bad-calls",title:{id:"gpEy6j",message:"Which calls should I review for this agent?"},userMessage:{id:"Mjxu9W",message:"Look through this agent's recent conversations and surface the calls most likely to be failures: short calls, early hangups, failed evaluation criteria and tool errors. Summarise what went wrong."},contextInjection:"The user is viewing one agent's conversation history and wants to find the calls worth investigating. Walk them through: 1) Use list_conversations scoped to this agent_id, then sort/filter to surface signal: very short calls (early hangups), calls where call_successful is failure, and calls with tool errors, 2) Open a handful with get_conversation_detail and read the transcript + tool calls to identify the failure mode (wrong answer = KB gap, stuck in a node = vague edge condition, tool error = auth/timeout), 3) Group the failures into themes rather than listing every call — '4 of 10 failures were the agent unable to verify the caller' is more actionable than ten transcripts, 4) For each theme, recommend the concrete fix (KB content, prompt boundary, edge condition, tool config) and offer to apply it, 5) If evaluation criteria aren't configured, call_successful is meaningless — flag that first.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["list_conversations","get_conversation_detail","update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-history:summarise-patterns",title:{id:"h8IjhU",message:"What are callers asking about?"},userMessage:{id:"9oTask",message:"Read across this agent's recent conversations and tell me the most common caller intents, the questions it handles well, and the topics where it struggles or hands off."},contextInjection:"The user wants a thematic read of one agent's recent conversation history. Walk them through: 1) Sample a meaningful set of recent conversations via list_conversations for this agent_id (not just the first page) and read transcripts with get_conversation_detail, 2) Cluster by caller intent — what are people actually calling about? Rank by frequency, 3) Separate intents the agent resolves cleanly from ones where it stalls, gives wrong info, or escalates, 4) Cross-reference struggling intents against the knowledge base and workflow — is the content missing, or is it present but not being retrieved/used?, 5) Turn the analysis into a prioritised improvement list: top unmet intents → new KB articles in Q&A format, recurring wrong answers → prompt/edge fixes, 6) Offer to draft the highest-impact KB article or prompt change.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["list_conversations","get_conversation_detail"]},{id:"agent-history:debug-one-call",title:{id:"HQATK8",message:"Help me debug a specific call"},userMessage:{id:"6EqBVz",message:"I'll point you at a conversation from this agent. Walk me through exactly what happened turn by turn — where the agent went wrong, which tool or node failed, and what I should change."},contextInjection:"The user wants to debug a single conversation from this agent's history. Walk them through: 1) Load the conversation with get_conversation_detail and read it turn by turn — transcript, tool calls, tool responses, node transitions, and the final evaluation, 2) Pinpoint the exact divergence: did the agent answer wrong (KB/prompt), call the wrong tool or get a tool error (auth/timeout/bad params), transition to the wrong workflow node (vague edge condition), or fail an evaluation criterion that was too strict?, 3) Trace the root cause to a specific config location (system prompt, a node's additional_prompt, an edge condition, a tool's schema), 4) Recommend the precise change and, if the user confirms, apply it via update_agent_config / update_tool, 5) Suggest a test case that would have caught this so it doesn't regress.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["get_conversation_detail","update_agent_config","update_tool","create_test"]}],se=[{id:"agent-users:repeat-callers",title:{id:"xZ7F5U",message:"Who keeps calling back?"},userMessage:{id:"1lRT0i",message:"Find this agent's repeat end-users — people who called multiple times, especially about the same issue — and tell me what's not getting resolved on the first call."},contextInjection:"The user is viewing the end-users of one agent and wants to find repeat callers, which usually signal unresolved issues. Walk them through: 1) Identify users with multiple conversations against this agent, 2) For the top repeat callers, read their conversations chronologically with get_conversation_detail — are they calling about the SAME unresolved issue, or different things?, 3) Same-issue repeats are the highest-value signal: the agent isn't actually resolving the problem (missing KB content, a workflow that dead-ends, or a tool that fails silently), 4) Watch for escalation patterns: short calls → longer calls → transfers to human means frustration is building, 5) Turn findings into fixes: add the missing answer to the knowledge base, close the workflow gap, or fix the failing tool — and offer to apply the change.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["list_conversations","get_conversation_detail"]},{id:"agent-users:analyse-one-user",title:{id:"2C4amY",message:"What can I learn from one user's history?"},userMessage:{id:"FOfGjA",message:"I'll point you at an end-user. Walk me through their full history with this agent — call frequency, outcomes, repeat issues and any signs of escalation — and turn it into agent improvements."},contextInjection:"The user wants to analyse a single end-user's history with this agent. Walk them through: 1) Pull this user's conversations and read them in order with get_conversation_detail, 2) Look at frequency, duration, and outcomes over time — is the relationship improving or degrading?, 3) Check whether they repeat the same request and whether the agent ever actually resolved it, 4) Note any transfers to human and what triggered them, 5) Check data collection consistency across calls — is the user giving consistent info, or is the agent re-asking things it already captured?, 6) Convert the analysis into concrete agent changes (KB content, prompt boundary, workflow fix) and offer to apply the top one.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["list_conversations","get_conversation_detail"]}],st=[{id:"agent-config:advanced:turn-settings",title:{id:"RBVBhj",message:"How do I tune turn-taking?"},userMessage:{id:"V31X6G",message:"Help me adjust conversation turn-taking for my agent. Cover turn mode, eagerness, interruption handling and turn timeout, and recommend settings for my channel."},contextInjection:"The user wants to adjust conversation turn-taking. Walk them through: 1) Turn mode: 'turn' (default, agent responds after detecting end of user's turn — use for conversations) vs 'silence' (agent responds after a silence threshold — use for specific scenarios only). Only 'turn' and 'silence' are valid — not 'conversational', 2) Turn eagerness: higher = agent responds faster (good for quick Q&A), lower = more patient (good for users who pause to think or speak slowly). Phone support typically needs lower eagerness than web, 3) Interruption handling: should the agent stop speaking when the user talks over it? Usually yes for telephony, but disable for IVR navigation where background audio triggers false interruptions, 4) Turn timeout (turn_timeout): how long the agent waits for user input before re-engaging — default 15s. Lower for fast-paced calls, higher for calls where the user may need time to look things up, 5) WARNING: per-node turn_timeout overrides can be set via API but the dashboard UI strips them on save — if using per-node config, deploy and maintain exclusively via API.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-config:advanced:asr-settings",title:{id:"6d3MPf",message:"How do I improve speech recognition accuracy?"},userMessage:{id:"usKsVE",message:"Help me improve ASR accuracy for my agent. Cover ASR quality settings, audio format quirks per channel, ASR keywords, language config and handling garbled transcripts."},contextInjection:"The user wants to improve speech recognition accuracy. Walk them through: 1) ASR quality: 'high' is recommended for production agents, 2) Audio format matters: telephony via Twilio uses ulaw_8000 (noisy, lower VAD thresholds) which produces more garbled transcripts than web (pcm_16000) or SIP (pcm_48000), 3) ASR keywords: add domain-specific terms the model wouldn't normally expect — industry acronyms (BIN, PCN, NPI, PBM), proper nouns (payer names, drug names, company names), and commonly confused number pairs (fifteen/fifty, thirteen/thirty). Do NOT add basic vocabulary that's already in the core model, 4) Language configuration: set the primary language in agent settings and ensure it matches the expected caller language, 5) For garbled transcripts on telephony: add prompt instructions to call skip_turn when transcript looks like noise/nonsense rather than saying 'I didn't understand' — garbled text is usually background noise or hold music, not speech directed at the agent, 6) Timeout behavior: when the user says nothing, the platform injects '...' as the transcript — this is distinct from garbled text.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config"]},{id:"agent-config:advanced:hold-silence",title:{id:"3mVqCN",message:"How do I handle holds and silence on calls?"},userMessage:{id:"AoglQH",message:"Help me make my telephony agent handle being placed on hold, long silence, and IVR prompts properly using skip_turn and silence timeout settings."},contextInjection:"The user's telephony agent needs to handle being placed on hold, silence, or IVR prompts. Walk them through: 1) The agent CANNOT detect hold music from audio — VAD treats music as speech (high VAD scores) and there's no hold-music classifier. The LLM only sees raw ASR transcript, which during hold music is garbled/meaningless/repetitive text, 2) Add skip_turn tool to the agent and instruct: 'If the transcript appears to be noise, hold music, or automated messages, call skip_turn and wait silently. Do not say hello or attempt to speak until the transcript clearly shows a human speaking', 3) skip_turn is critical because it disables BOTH turn_timeout (agent re-engagement) and silence_end_call_timeout (platform auto-disconnect) for that turn — without it, the agent will either start talking during the hold or the platform will disconnect, 4) Check silence_end_call_timeout setting: if enabled (default is disabled/-1), extended silence WILL disconnect the call unless skip_turn is active. For agents that may be on hold, either disable this or ensure reliable skip_turn usage, 5) Anti-narration guardrail: add 'Never narrate internal actions — everything you say is spoken aloud on the call. Do not say I need to wait or Let me look that up' to the prompt, 6) For multiple consecutive garbled turns: politely ask the other party to repeat.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config","create_tool"]},{id:"agent-config:advanced:fix-pronunciation",title:{id:"fbZGtQ",message:"Why is my agent mispronouncing numbers and words?"},userMessage:{id:"Fnzm0V",message:"Help me fix mispronounced words, garbled numbers and bad output from my agent. Tailor the fix to my TTS model (V3 vs V2) and cover digit grouping, pacing and anti-markup rules."},contextInjection:"The user's agent is mispronouncing words, reading numbers wrong, or producing garbled output. Walk them through the fix based on their TTS model: 1) V3 (eleven_v3_conversational): number normalization is entirely prompt-driven. Add explicit instructions: 'Read phone numbers digit by digit in groups of 3-3-4 with pauses: five five five, one two three, four five six seven', 'Spell email addresses: j o h n at gmail dot com', 'Say dash as a word for codes: C H dash five zero zero zero one', 2) V3 critical rule: never output HTML tags, SSML tags, markdown, or any markup — V3 reads EVERYTHING aloud including <br>, <break>, **bold**. Add anti-markup instructions to the prompt, 3) V2 models (eleven_flash_v2/v2_5): the TTS normalizer handles most numbers automatically, but add prompt instructions for domain-specific formats, 4) For digit-heavy fields (account numbers, SSNs, NPIs): instruct the LLM to break into groups of 3-4 with pauses and read digit-by-digit, not as whole numbers (never 'fifteen hundred' for 1500), 5) Pacing: SSML <break time=\"0.5s\"/> is most reliable for pauses, then punctuation (commas, em-dashes), then V3 audio tags like [slow], 6) For varied correction acknowledgments: add alternatives to the persona section ('Oh, got it', 'Sorry about that', 'Let me fix that') instead of robotic 'Thank you for that correction' every time.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["update_agent_config"]}],sa=[{id:"agent-config:branches:ab-test",title:{id:"SVNn8y",message:"How do I safely test changes to a live agent?"},userMessage:{id:"DOfXr2",message:"Help me set up an A/B test on my agent. Walk me through creating a branch, allocating traffic, monitoring performance and rolling out winning changes safely."},contextInjection:"The user wants to A/B test agent configurations. Walk them through: 1) Create a new branch from the current main branch — this clones the entire agent config, 2) Make changes to the branch (different prompt, LLM, voice, workflow, etc.) without affecting production, 3) Use 'Edit traffic split' to allocate a percentage of live traffic to the branch (e.g., 90% main / 10% test), 4) Traffic split must total 100% across all active branches, 5) Monitor branch performance in the evaluation dashboard — compare success rates, latency, and user satisfaction across branches, 6) Once a branch proves better: increase its traffic share gradually (10% → 50% → 100%), or merge it back to main, 7) Keep tests focused — change one variable at a time (prompt OR model OR voice) so you can attribute performance differences to the specific change.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["create_branch","set_traffic_split","list_branches"]},{id:"agent-config:branches:manage-traffic",title:{id:"nqrw8X",message:"How do I manage traffic splits across branches?"},userMessage:{id:"7cL6J8",message:"Help me manage traffic splits across my agent branches, including assigning weights, removing branches from traffic and rolling out safely."},contextInjection:"The user wants to manage how traffic is distributed across agent branches. Walk them through: 1) Click 'Edit traffic split' to open the deployment dialog, 2) Add branches to the traffic split and assign percentage weights — they must total 100%, 3) Only deployed (non-draft) branches can receive traffic, 4) To remove a branch from live traffic: set its weight to 0% or remove it from the split, 5) Changes take effect immediately after deploying — monitor closely after any traffic change, 6) For safe rollouts: start new branches at low traffic (5-10%) and increase gradually based on metrics, 7) You can view branch analytics to compare performance before adjusting splits.",priority:nz.High,recommendedTools:["list_branches","set_traffic_split"]},{id:"agent-config:branches:merge",title:{id:"QdVpRz",message:"How do I merge a branch back to main?"},userMessage:{id:"sGn6zh",message:"Walk me through merging a branch back to main, including pre-merge checks, what merging actually does (full overwrite, not diff), and how to roll back if needed."},contextInjection:"The user wants to merge a branch back to main. Walk them through: 1) Merging replaces the main branch config with the branch's config — this is a full overwrite, not a diff merge, 2) Before merging: ensure the branch has been tested with live traffic and metrics look good, 3) Protected branches require admin permissions to merge, 4) After merging: the branch still exists but its config is now identical to main — you can archive it to keep the branches list clean, 5) You cannot merge the current main branch or branches that are receiving live traffic (redirect traffic to main first), 6) If you need to roll back after a merge: check the branch history to restore a previous version.",priority:nz.Medium,recommendedTools:["list_branches","merge_branch_preview","merge_branch"]}],sn=[{id:"dashboard:needs-improvement",title:{id:"IVMbPX",message:"Which of my agents need attention first?"},userMessage:{id:"ZUqlRH",message:"Help me identify which of my agents need attention first based on success rates, latency, drop-off and traffic, and recommend where to start."},contextInjection:"The user wants to identify which agents need attention. Walk them through: 1) Look at per-agent success rates and identify outliers — which agents have the lowest call_successful rates?, 2) Check latency metrics — any agents significantly slower than others? Consider their LLM model choice (nano/lite models are fast but unreliable for complex tasks), 3) Review conversation drop-off rates — high drop-off suggests dead air (too many workflow transitions), tool timeouts, or poor turn-taking settings, 4) For each underperforming agent: check the prompt quality (is it structured with sections?), LLM choice (is it capable enough for the task?), tool configuration (are tools scoped correctly?), and knowledge base coverage (are key answers missing?), 5) Prioritize fixes by impact: highest traffic agents first, then agents with the worst success rates.",priority:nz.High},{id:"dashboard:llm-costs",title:{id:"C7SR6F",message:"How do I cut my LLM costs?"},userMessage:{id:"QyHqAD",message:"Help me reduce my LLM spend across agents. Identify the biggest spenders, find places to downgrade models, shorten prompts and cut workflow nodes."},contextInjection:"The user wants to reduce their LLM costs. Walk them through: 1) Break down cost per model and per agent to find the biggest spenders — gpt-4o is ~$0.037/min, claude-sonnet-4.6 is ~$0.045/min, while gemini-2.5-flash is ~$0.002/min, 2) Identify agents using expensive models for simple tasks — a basic FAQ agent doesn't need gpt-4o ($0.037/min) when gpt-4o-mini ($0.002/min) or gemini-2.5-flash ($0.002/min) would work, 3) Shorten system prompts to reduce token usage — every token in the prompt is processed on every turn, 4) Optimize workflows: fewer nodes = fewer cascade restarts = less LLM usage. Merge sequential phases into single nodes with internal phase labels, 5) Check cascade configuration: if the primary model frequently times out, the cascade runs two models — consider a faster primary to avoid double-billing, 6) For high-volume agents: consider gemini-2.5-flash as the primary model — best cost/quality ratio at ~$0.002/min with 1.05s latency.",priority:nz.Medium}],ss=[{id:"agents-list:first-agent",title:{id:"5k8kgl",message:"How do I set up my first agent?"},userMessage:{id:"4dNz7b",message:"Walk me through setting up my first agent end to end: choosing a starting point, writing the system prompt, picking an LLM and voice, configuring the first message, adding end_call, testing, and deploying."},contextInjection:"The user is new and wants to set up their first agent. Walk them through: 1) Choose a starting point: template (faster) or blank agent (full control), 2) Write a system prompt with clear sections: # Personality (name, role, 2-3 traits), # Tone (concise, professional), # Guardrails (what the agent must never do), 3) Select an LLM — recommend gemini-2.5-flash for starting out (fast, cheap, capable), 4) Pick a voice from the voice library — for English, use eleven_flash_v2 as TTS model (lowest latency), 5) Set first_message based on channel: inbound telephony → 'Thank you for calling [company], how can I help?', web widget → 'Hi! I'm here to help with [topic]. What can I do for you?', 6) Add an end_call system tool — every agent needs this, 7) Test in the built-in playground with realistic scenarios: happy path, off-topic question, request for human, 8) Deploy: widget embed for web, phone number for telephony, or API/SDK for custom integration.",priority:nz.High,condition:e=>!0===e.isFirstAgent}],si=[{id:"history:analyse-conversations",title:{id:"9waObe",message:"What patterns are in my conversation history?"},userMessage:{id:"jWHaYh",message:"Help me analyse my conversation history for patterns and insights: recurring failures, drop-off points, data extraction gaps and per-agent performance differences."},contextInjection:"The user wants to analyse their conversation history for patterns and insights. Help them by: 1) Use the filters to narrow down to a meaningful set — by agent, date range, call result (successful/failed), or duration, 2) Look for recurring failure patterns: are calls failing at the same workflow node? Are users consistently asking questions the agent can't answer?, 3) Check average call duration — unusually short calls may indicate the agent isn't engaging, unusually long calls may indicate the agent is stuck in loops, 4) Review data collection completeness — are key fields consistently null? That indicates extraction criteria need improvement, 5) Look at drop-off points — where in the conversation do users hang up? Dead air during node transitions is a common cause, 6) Compare performance across agents or branches to identify which configurations work best, 7) Export insights to improve prompt engineering, knowledge base content, and workflow design.",priority:nz.High},{id:"history:debug-call",title:{id:"fbIB0O",message:"How do I debug a specific conversation?"},userMessage:{id:"W7u_o4",message:"Help me debug a specific conversation. Walk me through reading the transcript, checking tool call logs, tracing workflow node visits and spotting common telephony issues."},contextInjection:"The user wants to debug a specific conversation. Walk them through: 1) Find the conversation using filters (date, agent, call result, duration), 2) Review the full transcript — look for where the conversation diverged from the expected flow, 3) Check tool call logs: did tools return errors? Did the agent call the right tool with the right parameters? Were tool responses parsed correctly?, 4) For workflow agents: check which nodes were visited and in what order — if the agent skipped a node, check edge conditions (too vague?) and tool scoping (tools available in wrong node?), 5) Look for common telephony issues: garbled transcripts (background noise), '...' entries (silence/timeout), mid-sentence cutoffs (transition during generation), 6) Check data collection results: were the right fields extracted? null values indicate the data wasn't found in the transcript — check if the extraction description is specific enough, 7) Make targeted fixes (prompt wording, edge conditions, tool config) and test with a similar scenario.",priority:nz.High},{id:"history:other-user-conversations",title:{id:"pbksu3",message:"Has this caller called before?"},userMessage:{id:"NsgL1z",message:"Help me find other conversations from the same caller or user, and review their full history to see if it's a repeat issue."},contextInjection:"The user wants to find other conversations from the same caller or user. Walk them through: 1) Note the caller ID, phone number, or user identifier from the current conversation, 2) Use the conversation history filters to search by that identifier — filter by phone number for telephony or user ID for widget/API conversations, 3) Review the user's conversation history chronologically to understand their full journey — are they calling back about the same issue? Did a previous call end prematurely?, 4) Check if the user's issue was resolved across multiple calls or if they're stuck in a loop, 5) Use the Users page (/app/agents/users) for a dedicated view of per-user conversation history.",priority:nz.Medium},{id:"history:filter-failures",title:{id:"H1mEdD",message:"Where are my failed conversations?"},userMessage:{id:"qgEBep",message:"Help me find and triage failed conversations. Walk me through filtering, identifying the most common failure modes, and prioritising fixes by frequency."},contextInjection:"The user wants to find conversations that failed or had issues. Walk them through: 1) Use the call result filter to show only failed or incomplete conversations, 2) Sort by duration — very short calls (under 30s) often indicate connection issues, agent errors, or immediate hangups, 3) Check for patterns: are failures concentrated on a specific agent, time period, or phone number range?, 4) For each failing conversation: review the transcript to identify the root cause — common causes include CascadeBrainError (all LLMs timed out), tool failures, workflow transitions getting stuck, or garbled ASR transcripts, 5) Group similar failures and prioritize fixes by frequency — fixing the most common failure mode has the highest impact.",priority:nz.High}],sr=[{id:"conversation:debug",title:{id:"VU3NrY",message:"What went wrong in this conversation?"},userMessage:{id:"DxWvFa",message:"Help me debug this conversation step by step: what diverged from the expected flow, tool call issues, workflow transitions and telephony quirks."},contextInjection:"The user is viewing a specific conversation and wants to debug it. Walk them through: 1) Review the full transcript — look for where the conversation diverged from the expected flow, 2) Check tool call logs: did tools return errors? Did the agent call the right tool with the right parameters? Were tool responses parsed correctly?, 3) For workflow agents: check which nodes were visited and in what order — if the agent skipped a node, check edge conditions (too vague?) and tool scoping (tools available in wrong node?), 4) Look for common telephony issues: garbled transcripts (background noise), '...' entries (silence/timeout), mid-sentence cutoffs (transition during generation), 5) Check data collection results: were the right fields extracted? null values indicate the data wasn't found in the transcript — check if the extraction description is specific enough, 7) Make targeted fixes (prompt wording, edge conditions, tool config) and test with a similar scenario.",priority:nz.High},{id:"conversation:improve-agent",title:{id:"rnw74H",message:"What should I change about my agent based on this call?"},userMessage:{id:"_SA2vY",message:"Use this conversation to suggest concrete agent improvements: prompt changes, knowledge base gaps, tool config tweaks, edge condition tightening, or tone adjustments."},contextInjection:"The user wants to use insights from this conversation to improve their agent. Walk them through: 1) Identify the specific failure or suboptimal behavior in the transcript — wrong answer, missed tool call, bad transition, awkward phrasing, 2) For wrong answers: check if the knowledge base has the correct information — if not, add it. If it does, check RAG retrieval (question-style headings, chunk size, max_vector_distance), 3) For missed tool calls: verify the tool description clearly explains when to use it, and check tool scoping (is it available in the right node?), 4) For bad transitions: tighten edge conditions with specific tool result references and verbal confirmations, 5) For tone/phrasing issues: update the system prompt personality and tone sections with explicit examples of desired responses, 6) For repeated questions after transitions: add 'Review conversation history — do not re-collect information already provided' to node prompts, 7) After making changes, run the agent tests or replay a similar scenario to verify the fix.",priority:nz.High},{id:"conversation:understand-evaluation",title:{id:"4PEa6H",message:"Why did this call fail evaluation?"},userMessage:{id:"h5uCzJ",message:"Explain why this conversation failed its evaluation criteria. Help me decide whether the failure is real or a false positive from a strict criterion, and what to fix."},contextInjection:"The user is looking at a conversation that failed evaluation criteria and wants to understand why. Walk them through: 1) Check which evaluation criteria failed — each criterion is an independent LLM assessment of the transcript, 2) Review the transcript against the failed criterion — was the agent actually at fault, or is the criterion too strict?, 3) Common false failures: criterion expects a specific phrase but the agent used an equivalent phrasing, criterion marks FAILURE when the call ended early (should use UNKNOWN), criterion is ambiguous and the evaluator LLM interpreted it differently than intended, 4) If the failure is real: identify what the agent should have done differently and fix the root cause (prompt, workflow, tool config), 5) If the failure is a false positive: refine the evaluation criterion to be more specific — add explicit guidance for edge cases and early termination.",priority:nz.Medium},{id:"conversation:check-caller-history",title:{id:"dR52QV",message:"Has this caller talked to us before?"},userMessage:{id:"xXpDJ9",message:"Help me find this caller's other conversations and understand their full history with our agents."},contextInjection:"The user wants to find other conversations from the same caller. Walk them through: 1) Note the caller ID, phone number, or user identifier from this conversation, 2) Use the conversation history filters to search by that identifier — filter by phone number for telephony or user ID for widget/API conversations, 3) Review the user's conversation history chronologically to understand their full journey — are they calling back about the same issue? Did a previous call end prematurely?, 4) Check if the user's issue was resolved across multiple calls or if they're stuck in a loop, 5) Use the Users page (/app/agents/users) for a dedicated view of per-user conversation history.",priority:nz.Medium}],so=[{id:"agent-testing:write-tests",title:{id:"ikD0vw",message:"Can you draft test cases for my agent?"},userMessage:{id:"r9BnI3",message:"Generate a starter set of test cases for my agent covering the happy path, edge cases, workflow transitions and tool failures, along with crisp evaluation criteria."},contextInjection:"The user wants to create test cases for their agent. Walk them through: 1) Start with the happy path — the most common successful conversation flow, 2) Add edge cases: caller says something off-topic, asks to speak to a human, gives invalid input, hangs up mid-flow, 3) For workflow agents: test each node transition — does the agent move to the right node at the right time? Does it get stuck?, 4) For tool-dependent agents: test tool success, tool failure, and tool timeout scenarios, 5) Define clear evaluation criteria — use the 'prompt' type with specific questions like 'Did the agent collect all required fields?' rather than vague goals like 'Was the call successful?', 6) For telephony: test with realistic audio conditions — background noise, interruptions, garbled speech, 7) Set up a regular testing cadence after making configuration changes.",priority:nz.High},{id:"agent-testing:debug-failures",title:{id:"JRvFM6",message:"Why are my tests failing?"},userMessage:{id:"oCqlt4",message:"Help me debug my failing tests step by step. Look at where the agent diverged, whether the failure is in content, tools or flow, and whether evaluation criteria are too strict."},contextInjection:"The user has tests that are failing. Debug step by step: 1) Review the test transcript to see exactly where the agent diverged from expectations, 2) Check if the failure is in the agent's response content, tool usage, or conversation flow, 3) For workflow agents: check if the agent transitioned to the wrong node — look for edge conditions that are too vague (e.g., 'Customer is identified' should be 'client_lookup tool returned a matching record'), 4) Check if evaluation criteria are too strict — criteria run post-call via separate LLM calls, so overly specific criteria may fail on valid conversations, 5) If the agent skips workflow steps: check for tool scoping issues (set tool_ids: [] at base, use additional_tool_ids per node) and add boundary instructions to each node, 6) Fix the root cause and re-run to verify.",priority:nz.High},{id:"agent-testing:organize-tests",title:{id:"a_IDB9",message:"How should I organise my test suite?"},userMessage:{id:"hNf3Pp",message:"Help me organise my test cases: folder structure, naming, smoke vs regression vs feature tests, and a workflow for running them on every change."},contextInjection:"The user wants to organize their test cases. Walk them through: 1) Use folders to group tests by function — e.g., 'Happy Path', 'Edge Cases', 'Tool Failures', 'Workflow Transitions', 2) Name tests descriptively — 'Caller asks for refund but past policy window' not 'Test 5', 3) For workflow agents: create a folder per node with tests covering entry, exit, and boundary conditions, 4) Separate regression tests (things that broke before) from feature tests (testing new capabilities), 5) Keep a 'Smoke Tests' folder with the 3-5 most important tests that should pass on every deploy, 6) Run the full suite after any agent configuration change — failed tests indicate regressions.",priority:nz.Medium},{id:"agent-testing:run-tests",title:{id:"9QxRaP",message:"How do I read my test results?"},userMessage:{id:"2HPtYN",message:"Help me run my tests and interpret the results: pass/fail per criterion, flaky tests, full-suite failures and tracking improvement across runs."},contextInjection:"The user wants help running tests and understanding results. Walk them through: 1) Select tests to run — individual tests, a folder, or the full suite, 2) Results show pass/fail for each evaluation criterion within each test, 3) A test passes only if ALL criteria pass — review individual criterion results to find which one failed, 4) For flaky tests (sometimes pass, sometimes fail): check if evaluation criteria are too subjective — tighten them with specific observable evidence, 5) If ALL tests fail simultaneously: check for agent-level issues (broken LLM config, expired API keys, invalid voice) rather than test-specific problems, 6) Compare results across test runs to track improvement — are you fixing more tests than you're breaking?",priority:nz.Medium}],sl=[{id:"dashboard:evaluation:improve-success",title:{id:"IUpjzN",message:"How do I lift my success rates?"},userMessage:{id:"27vYdY",message:"Help me improve my agents' success rates. Identify the most-failed evaluation criteria, look for patterns in failing conversations, and recommend the highest-impact fixes."},contextInjection:"The user is viewing evaluation analytics and wants to improve success rates. Walk them through: 1) Identify which evaluation criteria fail most often — focus fixes on the most common failure, 2) Review failing conversations to find patterns — are failures concentrated on specific agents, times, or user types?, 3) For workflow agents: check if failures correlate with specific node transitions — vague edge conditions cause the agent to skip or repeat steps, 4) Check if evaluation criteria are too strict or ambiguous — criteria that fail on valid conversations waste your attention, 5) For each common failure: trace to the root cause (prompt wording, tool config, knowledge base gap) and fix it, then re-run tests to verify.",priority:nz.High},{id:"dashboard:evaluation:setup-criteria",title:{id:"Z_W3Nr",message:"How do I write good evaluation criteria?"},userMessage:{id:"UMFiG5",message:"Help me define evaluation criteria for post-call analysis. Cover writing them as questions, being specific about success/failure, handling early termination and grouping checks."},contextInjection:"The user wants to define evaluation criteria for post-call analysis. Walk them through: 1) Write criteria as evaluation questions, not agent instructions — 'Evaluate whether the agent collected all required fields' not 'The agent should collect all fields', 2) Be specific about success/failure: 'Mark SUCCESS only if the agent read the disclosure starting with [exact phrase]' not 'Did the agent read the disclosure?', 3) Always include UNKNOWN guidance for early termination: 'If the call ended before reaching this phase, mark UNKNOWN rather than FAILURE', 4) Keep to 3-5 criteria covering: compliance, completeness, accuracy, and boundaries, 5) Each criterion is a separate LLM call — keep criteria concise but specific.",priority:nz.High}],sc=[{id:"dashboard:data-collection:setup",title:{id:"N33UFn",message:"How do I extract structured data from calls?"},userMessage:{id:"oVOSCl",message:"Help me set up post-call data collection. Cover field types, when to use enums, why fields can't reference each other, and designing for null when calls end early."},contextInjection:"The user wants to extract structured data from conversations. Walk them through the data_collection configuration: 1) Define fields with clear extraction instructions in the description, 2) Choose the right type: boolean for yes/no, string with enum for categories, integer/number for amounts, 3) Use enum liberally — even large enums give stronger guidance than descriptions alone, 4) Each field is extracted independently — never reference other fields in descriptions, 5) Never request comma-separated multi-value responses — the platform only supports scalar types, 6) Design for null: if the caller hangs up early, most fields will be null — that's expected.",priority:nz.High},{id:"dashboard:data-collection:fix-extraction",title:{id:"CcLZiv",message:"Why isn't my data extraction working?"},userMessage:{id:"Bc3WWL",message:"Help me debug data collection fields that aren't extracting. Check field descriptions, types, enum coverage, and whether the target data actually appears in transcripts."},contextInjection:"The user's data collection fields aren't extracting correctly. Debug step by step: 1) Check if the field description clearly tells the extraction LLM what to look for — vague descriptions produce unreliable results, 2) Verify the field type matches the expected data — using 'string' for a yes/no field produces 'yes'/'no' strings instead of true/false booleans, 3) For enum fields: check that the enum values cover all realistic possibilities — missing values cause null results, 4) Review conversation transcripts to see if the target data actually appears — null extraction is expected when the information was never discussed, 5) Keep descriptions under ~500 characters — long descriptions dilute the instruction, 6) Each field is independent — the extraction for field A cannot see field B's result.",priority:nz.High}],sd=[{id:"dashboard:tools:reduce-failures",title:{id:"b4OSjY",message:"Why are my tools failing so often?"},userMessage:{id:"3bMiDW",message:"Help me reduce tool call failures. Identify which tools fail most and walk me through fixing webhook errors, timeouts, wrong calls, missing calls and bad parameters."},contextInjection:"The user is looking at tool analytics and wants to reduce failure rates. Walk them through: 1) Identify which tools fail most — is it a single tool or widespread?, 2) For webhook tool failures: check the endpoint URL accessibility, authentication headers (expired keys/tokens?), and request body format, 3) For timeout failures: increase response_timeout_secs (default 20s, max 120s) if the API is slow, 4) For wrong tool calls: improve the tool description — the LLM uses it to decide when to call the tool, 5) For missing tool calls: check tool scoping — is the tool available in the right workflow node?, 6) For tools called with wrong parameters: check parameter descriptions and ensure they clearly describe expected values.",priority:nz.High},{id:"dashboard:tools:optimize-latency",title:{id:"6K2hbJ",message:"Why are my tool calls slow?"},userMessage:{id:"b8yE4v",message:"Help me reduce tool call latency. Identify the slowest tools, decide whether to migrate MCP tools to webhooks, and look for ways to merge sequential tool chains."},contextInjection:"The user wants to reduce tool call latency. Walk them through: 1) Check response times for each tool — which tools are the slowest?, 2) For slow webhook tools: optimize the downstream API or increase server capacity, 3) For MCP tools: MCP adds protocol overhead — for latency-sensitive voice agents, consider migrating critical tools to webhook format, 4) Reduce tool call chains — if the agent calls tool A, then tool B, then tool C sequentially, the latency compounds. Consider merging into a single endpoint, 5) Set response_timeout_secs appropriately — too high wastes time on dead endpoints, too low causes false timeouts.",priority:nz.High}],su=[{id:"dashboard:llms:compare-models",title:{id:"yNQpbZ",message:"Which LLM is performing best for me?"},userMessage:{id:"Ya77np",message:"Help me compare LLM performance across my agents: latency, cascade fallback rates, error rates and provider diversity, and recommend changes."},contextInjection:"The user is viewing LLM analytics and wants to compare model performance. Walk them through: 1) Compare latency across models — gpt-4o-mini (~736ms) and gpt-4.1-mini (~834ms) are fastest, gemini-2.5-flash (~1.05s) is best value, claude-sonnet-4.6 (~937ms) is best for complex reasoning, 2) Check cascade fallback rates — if the primary model frequently times out, the cascade fires two models (double cost), 3) For telephony: 3s cascade timeout is recommended — any model slower than that triggers fallbacks, 4) Check error rates per model — 429 rate limiting means you're hitting provider quotas, 5) Use cross-provider diversity in cascades (e.g., primary Gemini, backup OpenAI, backup Anthropic) to avoid correlated failures.",priority:nz.High},{id:"dashboard:llms:reduce-costs",title:{id:"C7SR6F",message:"How do I cut my LLM costs?"},userMessage:{id:"QyHqAD",message:"Help me reduce my LLM spend across agents. Identify the biggest spenders, find places to downgrade models, shorten prompts and cut workflow nodes."},contextInjection:"The user wants to reduce LLM costs. Walk them through: 1) Identify which agents/models cost the most — gpt-4o is ~$0.037/min while gemini-2.5-flash is ~$0.002/min, 2) Downgrade simple agents to mini models — a basic FAQ agent doesn't need gpt-4o, 3) Shorten system prompts — every token is processed on every turn, 4) Reduce workflow nodes — fewer cascade restarts means less LLM usage, 5) Check cascade timeouts — frequent fallbacks mean paying for two models per turn.",priority:nz.High}],sg=[{id:"dashboard:kb:improve-retrieval",title:{id:"kFB9QY",message:"How do I improve knowledge base retrieval?"},userMessage:{id:"qdQ7Xx",message:"Help me improve knowledge base retrieval: question-style headings, chunk size, deduping, embedding model choice and tuning max_vector_distance."},contextInjection:"The user is viewing knowledge base analytics and wants to improve retrieval. Walk them through: 1) Use question-style headings in documents — retrieval queries are rewritten as questions, so Q-style headings dramatically increase embedding similarity, 2) Keep articles to 1.5-3 KB for optimal chunking, 3) Minimize duplicate content across articles — duplicates waste retrieval slots, 4) Remove YAML front matter — it becomes noise in the first chunk, 5) Adjust max_vector_distance (default 0.6) — lower is stricter, higher retrieves more but less relevant, 6) For non-English content: use multilingual_e5_large_instruct embedding model.",priority:nz.High},{id:"dashboard:kb:add-content",title:{id:"GGoft6",message:"What content is missing from my knowledge base?"},userMessage:{id:"kgP9ML",message:"Help me figure out what knowledge base content to add. Look at conversation history for unanswered questions and recommend articles to write in Q&A format."},contextInjection:"The user wants guidance on what knowledge base content to add. Walk them through: 1) Review conversation history for questions the agent couldn't answer — these are your highest-priority content gaps, 2) Structure each article around a single question or topic, 3) Write in Q&A format with question-style headings, 4) Include the exact phrasing users actually use — not just internal terminology, 5) For product/service FAQs: cover pricing, availability, requirements, and common objections, 6) Keep articles self-contained — cross-references don't work since the LLM only sees retrieved chunks, not referenced articles.",priority:nz.High}],sm=[{id:"dashboard:advanced:understand-metrics",title:{id:"QemDsT",message:"What do these metrics mean?"},userMessage:{id:"O3jMsd",message:"Walk me through what each metric on this analytics view means: latency, success rate, call duration, drop-off and tool call success, and what good numbers look like."},contextInjection:"The user is viewing conversation-level analytics and wants to understand the metrics. Walk them through: 1) Average latency: time from user finishing speaking to agent starting to respond — includes ASR processing, LLM inference, and TTS generation. Below 1.5s is good for telephony, 2) Success rate: percentage of conversations where all evaluation criteria passed — requires evaluation criteria to be configured, 3) Call duration: average conversation length — compare against your expected flow duration to identify stuck agents or premature hangups, 4) Drop-off rate: where users abandon conversations — high drop-off during transitions indicates dead air from too many workflow nodes, 5) Tool call success rate: percentage of tool calls that return without errors — low rates indicate configuration issues, 6) Filter by date range and agent to isolate specific performance trends.",priority:nz.High},{id:"dashboard:advanced:export-data",title:{id:"rKkQtG",message:"How do I export conversation data?"},userMessage:{id:"Nx96C2",message:"Help me access conversation data programmatically: the API endpoints, pagination, filtering, and when to use post-call webhooks instead of polling."},contextInjection:"The user wants to export or access conversation data programmatically. Walk them through: 1) Use the API to fetch conversation history: GET /v1/convai/conversations for listing, GET /v1/convai/conversations/{id} for details, 2) Paginate with cursor-based pagination for large datasets, 3) Filter by agent_id, date range, and call result to narrow results, 4) Each conversation includes: transcript, tool calls, data collection results, evaluation scores, and metadata, 5) For bulk analysis: set up post-call webhooks to receive conversation data in real-time as calls complete — this is more efficient than polling the API, 6) Data retention: conversations are stored for your plan's retention period.",priority:nz.Medium}],sh=[{id:"phone:setup",title:{id:"hJrlZp",message:"How do I give my agent a phone number?"},userMessage:{id:"lB_n-f",message:"Walk me through setting up a phone number for my agent: picking or importing a number, assigning it, configuring the first message and key telephony settings, and testing."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user wants to set up a phone number for their agent. Walk them through: 1) Choose a number from available regions or import their own via Twilio/SIP, 2) Assign the number to their agent, 3) Configure the first_message for inbound telephony: brief greeting + purpose ('Thank you for calling [company], how can I help you?'), 4) Important telephony settings: audio format is ulaw_8000 for Twilio (noisier than web), ASR quality should be 'high', cascade_timeout should be 3s (dead air is unacceptable on phone), 5) Test with an inbound call — check for: greeting plays correctly, agent responds naturally, no excessive dead air between turns, 6) Set up fallback behavior: add transfer_to_number tool for human escalation, configure call limits to prevent abuse.",priority:nz.High},{id:"phone:troubleshoot",title:{id:"5pGQm1",message:"Why aren't my incoming calls working?"},userMessage:{id:"UTtTdB",message:"Help me troubleshoot incoming calls. Walk me through verifying the number assignment, checking agent status, reviewing call logs, ruling out cascade or TTS issues, and silence timeouts."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user is having issues with incoming calls. Debug step by step: 1) Verify the phone number is assigned to the correct agent in the phone numbers tab, 2) Check the agent's status — is it active with a valid LLM and voice configured?, 3) Test with a direct call to rule out carrier issues, 4) Review call logs for error codes: 'All LLMs Failed' (CascadeBrainError) means every model in the cascade timed out or returned 429 — check provider status and consider adding more cascade backups with cross-provider diversity, 5) Check if call limits or rate limiting are blocking calls, 6) If calls connect but agent is silent: check first_message is set, verify TTS model is valid for the language (eleven_flash_v2 is English-only), check voice_id is valid, 7) If agent produces garbled output: verify the TTS model — don't use eleven_turbo_v2_5 or eleven_flash_v2_5 for English-only agents, 8) If calls disconnect unexpectedly: check silence_end_call_timeout setting — if enabled, silent holds will disconnect the call.",priority:nz.Medium},{id:"phone:outbound",title:{id:"z3RlLi",message:"Can you make an outbound call for me?"},userMessage:{id:"qjH-nT",message:"Help me set up outbound calling, or trigger an outbound call now if I've named an agent and phone number. Cover the first message, IVR navigation, voicemail detection and hold handling."},contextInjection:"The user wants their agent to make outbound calls. You can trigger outbound calls directly using trigger_twilio_call or trigger_sip_call (depending on the phone number's provider) -- use list_phone_numbers first to find an outbound-capable number and its provider. If the user references a specific agent and phone number, trigger the call immediately (confirm before dialing). Walk them through outbound-specific considerations: 1) First message is critical for outbound -- it's the only guaranteed utterance before the human responds. Keep it under 2 sentences: 'Hi, this is [name] calling from [company] regarding your [topic]', 2) The human may talk over the greeting -- configure interruption handling appropriately, 3) For outbound to IVRs: add the play_keypad_touch_tone system tool for DTMF navigation. Configure with suppress_turn_after_dtmf: true and disable_interruptions: true. In the prompt: 'Send ALL digits in a single call as one string, insert w between groups of 2-4 digits for pauses (e.g., 123w456w7890). Never call this tool one digit at a time', 4) Add voicemail_detection system tool if the agent may reach voicemail, 5) Add skip_turn and hold/silence handling -- the agent may be placed on hold or encounter IVR prompts, 6) System variables available: system__caller_id for the outbound number.",priority:nz.Medium}],sp=[{id:"batch:setup",title:{id:"eD2XiN",message:"How do I run a batch calling campaign?"},userMessage:{id:"5FGdX4",message:"Walk me through setting up a batch calling campaign: CSV format, dynamic variables, retry settings, business hours, test batches and concurrent call limits."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user wants to set up a batch calling campaign. Walk them through: 1) Prepare the CSV call list with required format: phone number column plus optional custom variable columns (these populate {{dynamic_variables}} in the prompt — e.g., first_name, appointment_date), 2) Select the agent and outbound phone number to use, 3) Configure call settings: max retries for unanswered calls, time between retries, business hours window (avoid calling outside reasonable hours), 4) If using dynamic variables: ensure the agent's prompt references them correctly and that dynamic_variable_placeholders are defined with sensible defaults, 5) Run a small test batch (5-10 calls) first — review transcripts and data collection results before scaling, 6) Monitor the active campaign: check success rates, call durations, and drop-off patterns, 7) Set up concurrent call limits based on their plan capacity to avoid overloading.",priority:nz.High}],sf=[{id:"integrations:setup-mcp",title:{id:"8lVkJr",message:"How do I connect an MCP server?"},userMessage:{id:"vl_z0r",message:"Walk me through connecting an MCP server to my agent, including transport type, authentication, tool naming, schema limitations and approval policies."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user wants to connect an MCP (Model Context Protocol) server to their agent. Walk them through: 1) MCP servers provide standardized tool discovery — the platform automatically fetches available tools from the server and exposes them to the agent, 2) Configure the server URL (HTTPS required except localhost), transport type (SSE or STREAMABLE_HTTP), and authentication (secret_token for Bearer auth OR auth_connection for OAuth — mutually exclusive), 3) Tool naming: MCP tools are flattened into the agent's tool list with a prefix format of '{server_id}:{tool_name}' — this can confuse the LLM. Add a prompt instruction telling the agent the exact tool names it should use, 4) Schema limitations: the platform strips most JSON Schema keywords (pattern, minimum, maximum, enum, default, examples) — only type, properties, required, description, and items are kept. If your tools use discriminated unions (oneOf), they may silently disappear. Move constraints into the tool description instead, 5) Tool overrides: use tool_config_overrides to set per-tool execution_mode, disable_interruptions, or force_pre_tool_speech, 6) Approval policies: AUTO_APPROVE_ALL (default), REQUIRE_APPROVAL_ALL, or REQUIRE_APPROVAL_PER_TOOL — for sensitive tools, require approval, 7) LATENCY WARNING: MCP servers add extra latency vs webhook tools due to the protocol overhead. For voice agents where latency matters, prefer webhook tools for simple API calls. Use MCP when you need the standardized tool discovery or complex tool ecosystems, 8) Use environment variables ({{env__MCP_URL}}) in the server URL to switch between staging and production MCP servers without duplicating agents.",priority:nz.High},{id:"integrations:troubleshoot",title:{id:"Tfj8DZ",message:"Why isn't my integration working?"},userMessage:{id:"fRw2iY",message:"Help me troubleshoot a broken integration: checking status, re-authenticating expired tokens, verifying permissions, reading error logs, and validating webhook tool config."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user has an integration that isn't working. Debug step by step: 1) Check the integration status in the dashboard — is it showing connected or errored?, 2) Re-authenticate if the connection has expired — OAuth tokens and API keys can expire, 3) Verify the integration permissions are sufficient for the required operations, 4) Check for error messages in the integration logs, 5) Test with a simple action to isolate whether it's an auth, permissions, or configuration issue, 6) If using webhook tools to connect to the integration: check the tool's response_timeout_secs (default 20s, max 120s) — slow APIs may need a higher timeout, 7) Verify request_headers format — must be an object not an array, and auth headers should use secret_id references for sensitive values.",priority:nz.Medium},{id:"integrations:migrate-platform",title:{id:"D0X0eo",message:"How do I migrate from another voice AI platform?"},userMessage:{id:"BodCYN",message:"Help me migrate from another voice AI platform to ElevenLabs. Cover workflow structure differences, edge conventions, global behaviours, tool migration, and dynamic variable limitations."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user wants to migrate from another voice AI platform. Walk them through the key differences and migration steps: 1) WORKFLOW STRUCTURE: ElevenLabs uses 'Agent Nodes' for conversation phases and edge conditions on edges for branching (no separate branch nodes needed). Data Collection handles variable extraction across the agent. Transfer types use 'blind' (cold transfer) and 'conference' (warm transfer), 2) EDGES: ElevenLabs uses ONE edge per node pair with forward_condition + backward_condition — don't create separate edges for each direction, 3) GLOBAL BEHAVIORS: use system tools (end_call, transfer_to_number) in the root prompt — they work from any node without explicit edges, 4) TOOLS: migrate webhook tools by mapping the schema — URLs must be static (no {{variables}} in paths), properties need descriptions, 5) STATE/VARIABLES: Dynamic variables only work with external data (pre-call webhook, SDK, tool responses) — NOT with data extracted from conversation alone, 6) Start with the core flow, test, then add edge cases incrementally.",priority:nz.High}],sv=[{id:"settings:workspace-secrets",title:{id:"NYRitP",message:"How do workspace secrets work?"},userMessage:{id:"COl6TX",message:"Help me set up workspace secrets and use them across my agents' webhook tools and integrations, instead of pasting API keys into each tool's config."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:'The user is on the workspace settings page and wants to manage shared secrets. Walk them through: 1) Workspace secrets store credentials once and share them across every agent\'s tools and integrations — no need to paste an API key into each webhook tool, 2) Create a secret here, then reference it by secret_id in a tool\'s request_headers ({"secret_id": "..."}) rather than a plaintext value — this keeps keys out of the agent JSON, 3) The same secret can back many tools and MCP servers; update it in one place and every consumer picks up the new value, 4) For staging vs production values that differ, prefer environment variables (Developers tab) and reference {{env__VAR}} in the tool config — secrets are for credentials, env vars are for per-environment values, 5) Rotation: update the secret value, verify tools still authenticate, and nothing else needs to change because consumers reference it by id.',priority:nz.High},{id:"settings:post-call-webhook",title:{id:"Cxy3kI",message:"How do I set up a post-call webhook?"},userMessage:{id:"0WxjwS",message:"Walk me through configuring the workspace post-call webhook so I receive every conversation's transcript and data when calls end, and explain how agents can override it."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user wants to configure the workspace-default post-call webhook. Walk them through: 1) The post-call webhook fires when any conversation ends and delivers the transcript, tool calls, data collection results, and evaluation scores to your endpoint — far more efficient than polling the conversations API, 2) Set the workspace default here; it applies to every agent unless an individual agent overrides it on its own Security tab, 3) Your endpoint should return 200 quickly and process asynchronously — slow handlers risk retries/duplicates, so dedupe on conversation_id, 4) Verify the webhook signature to ensure requests genuinely come from ElevenLabs, 5) Use this for: CRM sync, analytics pipelines, QA review queues, and triggering follow-up actions, 6) The conversation-initiation client data webhook (also on this page) is the inbound counterpart — it runs when a Twilio/SIP call begins to inject dynamic variables before the agent speaks.",priority:nz.Medium},{id:"settings:auth-connections",title:{id:"L9HyQS",message:"How do I share OAuth connections across agents?"},userMessage:{id:"Wb5fnO",message:"Help me set up shared auth connections (like Google or Slack) at the workspace level so my agents' tools and integrations can reuse them without re-authenticating each time."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user wants to manage shared OAuth auth connections at the workspace level. Walk them through: 1) Auth connections store an OAuth grant (Google, Slack, etc.) once at the workspace level so multiple agents' tools and MCP servers can reuse it, 2) Create the connection here and complete the OAuth flow, then reference it from a webhook tool or MCP server's authentication (auth_connection) instead of a static secret_token — the two are mutually exclusive on a given tool, 3) For MCP servers requiring OAuth, this is the auth path; for simple Bearer-token APIs, a workspace secret is simpler, 4) If a connection expires or is revoked upstream, re-authenticate here once and every consuming tool recovers — no per-tool changes needed, 5) Scope the OAuth grant to only the permissions the tools actually need.",priority:nz.Medium},{id:"settings:api-keys",title:{id:"QztqKa",message:"How do I manage API keys safely?"},userMessage:{id:"FAvi5z",message:"Help me manage my API keys: naming, scoping, secure storage, using the secrets API for webhook tool auth, and rotating keys without breaking integrations."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user wants to manage their API keys. Walk them through: 1) Create a new API key with a descriptive name (e.g., 'production-backend', 'staging-testing'), 2) Set appropriate key permissions and scope — limit to only what's needed, 3) Store securely: environment variables for server-side apps, secrets manager for production (never commit to code repos), 4) For webhook tool auth: use the secrets API (GET /v1/convai/secrets) to store API keys server-side and reference them via secret_id in request_headers — this keeps secrets out of the agent JSON, 5) Key rotation: create the new key FIRST, update all integrations and webhook tools, verify they work, THEN revoke the old key, 6) Monitor key usage for anomalies — unexpected spikes may indicate a leaked key.",priority:nz.Medium}],sy=[{id:"env-vars:staging-vs-prod",title:{id:"3Rzqt-",message:"How do I run one agent for staging and prod?"},userMessage:{id:"Gp6wjk",message:"Help me use environment variables so a single agent works for both staging and production — different webhook URLs, MCP servers, headers and tool params per environment, without duplicating the agent."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user is on the environment variables page and wants one agent definition to serve both staging and production. Walk them through: 1) Environment variables let webhook URLs, MCP server URLs, auth headers, and tool parameters resolve to different values per environment — so you keep ONE agent instead of duplicating it, 2) Reference a variable anywhere in tool config with {{env__VARIABLE_NAME}} (webhook URLs, MCP URLs, request headers, tool params), 3) Three types: StringEnvironmentVariable (plain value), SecretEnvironmentVariable (sensitive, stored via secret_id), and AuthConnectionEnvironmentVariable (OAuth connection), 4) Create the same variable name in each environment with the environment-appropriate value, 5) At conversation start, set the environment parameter to control which set of values resolves, 6) Common pattern: {{env__API_BASE_URL}} in a webhook tool's URL → points at staging.api.example.com in staging, api.example.com in production.",priority:nz.High},{id:"env-vars:secrets-in-vars",title:{id:"S3uqEt",message:"How do I keep secrets out of my agent config?"},userMessage:{id:"iWvhQe",message:"Help me store sensitive values like API keys as secret environment variables and reference them in tools, so credentials never sit in plaintext in the agent JSON."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user wants to keep credentials out of their agent config using environment variables. Walk them through: 1) Use a SecretEnvironmentVariable (not a plain StringEnvironmentVariable) for anything sensitive — it stores the value behind a secret_id rather than in the agent JSON, 2) Reference it the same way as any env var, {{env__VARIABLE_NAME}}, in webhook URLs, headers, or MCP server config, 3) Difference vs workspace secrets: workspace secrets are a single shared value; secret env vars resolve to different values per environment (staging vs production), so use secret env vars when the credential itself differs by environment, 4) Rotation: update the secret env var's value for the relevant environment and every tool referencing {{env__VAR}} picks it up — no tool edits needed, 5) Never paste an API key directly into a tool's request_headers as a plaintext value when you can reference a secret instead.",priority:nz.Medium},{id:"env-vars:reference-in-tools",title:{id:"Qb53Qw",message:"Where can I use environment variables?"},userMessage:{id:"LGYmcc",message:"Show me exactly where the {{env__VAR}} syntax works in my agent's tools and integrations, and help me wire a variable into a webhook tool or MCP server."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user wants to know where environment variables can be referenced and how to wire one in. Walk them through: 1) {{env__VARIABLE_NAME}} resolves in: webhook tool URLs, webhook request headers, webhook tool parameters (constant values), and MCP server URLs/headers, 2) It does NOT work like a conversation dynamic variable — env vars resolve per environment at conversation start, not from conversation content or tool responses ({{dynamic_var}} is the separate mechanism for that), 3) To wire one in: create the variable here, then in the tool's config replace the hardcoded URL/header value with {{env__YOUR_VAR}}, 4) Verify by starting a conversation with the target environment set and checking the tool hits the expected endpoint, 5) Good candidates: base API URLs, MCP server hostnames, region/tenant identifiers, and per-environment auth tokens (as secret env vars).",priority:nz.Medium}],s_=[{id:"whatsapp:setup",title:{id:"hVv4la",message:"How do I connect my agent to WhatsApp?"},userMessage:{id:"msJCuz",message:"Walk me through connecting my agent to WhatsApp: Business Account setup, phone number verification, integration config, agent assignment and testing."},guideOnly:!0,contextInjection:"The user wants to connect their agent to WhatsApp. Walk them through: 1) Set up a WhatsApp Business Account if they don't have one — this requires Facebook Business verification, 2) Verify their business phone number through the WhatsApp Business API, 3) Connect the WhatsApp Business API to ElevenLabs via the integrations page, 4) Assign an agent to handle WhatsApp messages, 5) Configure the agent for text-based interaction: first_message should be welcoming for chat ('Hi! How can I help you today?'), response length should be concise for mobile reading, 6) WhatsApp is text-based like web widget — no telephony-specific concerns (DTMF, hold handling, ASR), but voice messages may be supported depending on configuration, 7) Test with a sample WhatsApp conversation before going live.",priority:nz.High}],sb=[{pageId:"dashboard",patterns:["/app/agents"],defaultTab:"general",triggers:sn,tabTriggers:{general:sn,evaluation:sl,"data-collection":sc,tools:sd,llms:su,"knowledge-base":sg,advanced:sm}},{pageId:"agents-list",patterns:["/app/agents/agents"],triggers:ss},{pageId:"agent-analytics",patterns:["/app/agents/agents/:agentId/analytics"],defaultTab:"general",tabTriggers:{general:n5,evaluation:n2,"data-collection":n3,tools:n8,llms:n4,"knowledge-base":n6,advanced:n7}},{pageId:"agent-history",patterns:["/app/agents/agents/:agentId/history/:path*"],triggers:n9},{pageId:"agent-users",patterns:["/app/agents/agents/:agentId/users/:path*"],triggers:se},{pageId:"agent-config-overview",patterns:["/app/agents/agents/:agentId"],triggers:[...n1,...n0]},{pageId:"agent-config-agent",patterns:["/app/agents/agents/:agentId/agent/:path*"],triggers:nW},{pageId:"agent-config-branches",patterns:["/app/agents/agents/:agentId/branches/:path*"],triggers:sa},{pageId:"agent-config-workflow",patterns:["/app/agents/agents/:agentId/workflow/:path*"],triggers:nJ},{pageId:"agent-config-knowledge-base",patterns:["/app/agents/agents/:agentId/knowledge-base/:path*"],triggers:nq},{pageId:"agent-config-analysis",patterns:["/app/agents/agents/:agentId/analysis/:path*"],triggers:nX},{pageId:"agent-config-coaching",patterns:["/app/agents/agents/:agentId/coaching/:path*"],triggers:nZ},{pageId:"agent-config-tools",patterns:["/app/agents/agents/:agentId/tools/:path*"],triggers:nG},{pageId:"agent-config-tests",patterns:["/app/agents/agents/:agentId/tests/:path*"],triggers:nY},{pageId:"agent-config-guardrails",patterns:["/app/agents/agents/:agentId/guardrails/:path*"],triggers:nQ},{pageId:"agent-config-settings",patterns:["/app/agents/agents/:agentId/settings/:path*"],defaultTab:"settings",tabTriggers:{"settings:security":n$,"settings:advanced":st},defaultSubtabs:{settings:"advanced"}},{pageId:"agent-testing",patterns:["/app/agents/agent-testing/:path*"],triggers:so},{pageId:"conversation-detail",patterns:["/app/agents/history/:conversationId/(details)?"],triggers:sr},{pageId:"history",patterns:["/app/agents/history"],triggers:si},{pageId:"knowledge-base",patterns:["/app/agents/knowledge-base/:path*"],triggers:nV},{pageId:"tools",patterns:["/app/agents/tools/:path*"],triggers:nK},{pageId:"phone-numbers",patterns:["/app/agents/phone-numbers/:path*"],triggers:sh},{pageId:"batch-calling",patterns:["/app/agents/batch-calling/:path*"],triggers:sp},{pageId:"integrations",patterns:["/app/agents/integrations/:path*"],triggers:sf},{pageId:"users",patterns:["/app/agents/users/:path*"],triggers:[{id:"users:analyse-user",title:{id:"Bwcgm8",message:"What patterns are in this user's calls?"},userMessage:{id:"JA80JL",message:"Help me analyse this end-user's full conversation history: call frequency, duration, outcomes, repeat issues and signs of escalation, and turn that into agent improvements."},contextInjection:"The user wants to analyse a specific end-user's conversation history. Walk them through: 1) Review the user's conversation list chronologically — look for patterns in call frequency, duration, and outcomes, 2) Check if the user is a repeat caller about the same issue — this indicates the agent isn't resolving their problem, 3) Look at which agents the user interacted with and whether they were transferred between agents, 4) Check data collection results across conversations — is the user providing consistent information?, 5) Identify if the user's issue escalated over time (short calls → longer calls → transfers to human), 6) Use these insights to improve the agent: add missing knowledge base content, fix workflow gaps, or adjust the prompt to handle this type of user better.",priority:nz.High},{id:"users:find-user",title:{id:"MBWKc_",message:"How do I find a specific user?"},userMessage:{id:"aIHR8R",message:"Help me find a specific end-user in the users list. Cover searching by phone number, user ID and metadata, and what to check if they don't show up."},contextInjection:"The user wants to find a specific end-user in the users list. Walk them through: 1) Use the search and filter options to find users by phone number, user ID, or other identifying information, 2) For telephony users: search by their caller ID or phone number, 3) For widget/API users: search by the user ID or metadata passed at conversation start, 4) Once found, click on the user to see all their conversations in one place, 5) If the user can't be found: check if conversations were from a different agent or time range, or if the user connected through a different channel.",priority:nz.Medium}]},{pageId:"settings",patterns:["/app/agents/settings/:path*"],triggers:sv},{pageId:"whatsapp",patterns:["/app/agents/whatsapp/:path*"],triggers:s_},{pageId:"environment-variables",patterns:["/app/developers/environment-variables/:path*"],triggers:sy}];e.s(["TRIGGER_REGISTRY",0,sb],679343)},624015,e=>{"use strict";var t=e.i(41264);let a={viewedTopic:null,isFullscreen:!1,agentId:null},n=(0,t.createExternalStore)(a);e.s(["clearTopicView",